(window.LING406_STUDY=window.LING406_STUDY||{})["m1"]={"revision":"ling406-study-1-m1","meta":{"module":"m1","exam":"Midterm 1","range":"Phonetics and phonology, morphemes and phrases, how brains work, brains and language, the Cognitive Revolution, speech errors, experimental design","lead":"These pages teach every Module I idea in course order, from speech sounds and morphemes through activation networks, aphasia, the Cognitive Revolution and speech production to experimental design. Each topic explains the reasoning behind the findings, and the review guide answers every prompt on the Midterm 1 review deck.","flags":["There is no answer key for LING406, so every answer here is written from the course material.","The pages m1-lecture04-p024 and m1-review-p021 credit the Interactive Activation model to Rumelhart and McClelland (1986), while m1-lecture04-p042 credits it to McClelland and Rumelhart (1981). The accepted dating is McClelland and Rumelhart (1981), so each page keeps its own wording here.","On m1-lecture04-p042 the text says that activation for WORK makes the last letter look more like an R than a K. WORK ends in K, and the letter output plot on the same page has k rising steadily while r stays low. The accepted reading is that word-level support for WORK pushes the ambiguous letter toward K.","On m1-lecture04-p015 the researchers are spelled Hubel and Weisel (1968). The accepted spelling is Hubel and Wiesel, and the cards keep the spelling given.","On m1-lecture09-p002 the text dates Oldfield and Wingfield to 1965, but the reference line on the same page cites Oldfield and Wingfield (1964) in Nature. Both papers exist, and the cards keep the 1965 date from the text.","On m1-lecture07-p009 the text says that whole clusters moving together would have yielded gish frotto. Moving the whole onsets f and gr would give grish fotto, while gish frotto exchanges only f and g. The Fromkin excerpt on the same page lists (6a) as fish grotto becoming frish gotto, and m1-lecture08-p011 later lists gish frotto as a sound-ordering error.","On m1-lecture06-p017 Chomsky's critique is said to predate Marr's work by about a decade. Chomsky's review appeared in 1959 and Marr's Type 1 and Type 2 discussion appeared in 1977, so the gap is closer to two decades.","On m1-lecture06-p003 Turing is said to have built decryption devices for the British Army. Accepted histories place his wartime codebreaking at Bletchley Park for the Government Code and Cypher School, a civilian agency, rather than the Army.","Balota and Lorch used lion as the prime and stripes as the target, as their abstract on m1-review-p025 states. The pages m1-lecture04-p040 and m1-review-p027 describe the reverse direction, stripes priming lion. The network account predicts both, but the tested direction was lion to stripes.","On m1-lecture05-p003 the phrenological faculty is spelled philopropogenitiveness. The accepted spelling is philoprogenitiveness.","On m1-lecture05-p014 the scan is described as H.M.'s middle temporal lobes. H.M.'s surgery removed parts of his medial temporal lobes, so the accepted term is medial temporal lobes, and the notes keep the wording given."]},"topics":[{"id":"m1-ipa-features","title":"Letters, the IPA and phonetic features","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p007","m1-lecture01-p008"],"main":"**Letters are not phonemes, so researchers need one standard system for writing the sounds of every language.** Each writing system has its own spelling conventions. Pinyin is perfectly regular for Mandarin, and French spelling is also very regular. An English reader still mispronounces “Wo qu tushuguan” or “Comment tu t’appelles?” because English letter habits do not carry over.","sub":["The **International Phonetic Alphabet** (IPA) gives one symbol per speech sound, so sounds can be compared directly across languages.","Its consonant chart is a system of comparisons, because a symbol’s position tells you how the sound is made."],"end":"So the IPA lets speech scientists talk precisely about how sounds in different languages are similar and different."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p009","m1-lecture01-p010","m1-lecture01-p013","m1-lecture01-p014"],"main":"**The consonant chart is organized by three features, place, manner and voicing.** **Place of articulation** (where the vocal tract closes or narrows) runs along the top from the front of the mouth to the back. **Manner of articulation** (how closed the passage is) runs down the side from fully closed to open. **Voicing** (when the vocal folds start vibrating relative to the closure) separates the members of each pair.","sub":["/p/ is a plosive, bilabial and not voiced. /b/ is a plosive, bilabial and voiced.","So /p/ and /b/ share manner and place and differ only in the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure.","The same voicing difference separates /t/ from /d/, so we can talk in general about “the voiced stops” /b/, /d/ and /g/.","The features are anchored to real movements of the vocal tract. Examples are both lips closing, the lower lip meeting the upper teeth, or air flowing through the nose."],"end":"So features let two sounds be described precisely as sharing two properties and differing in a third."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p011","m1-lecture01-p012"],"main":"**The same voicing contrast is produced differently across languages and contexts.** In English, the first sound of “peer” is often an unvoiced **aspirated** stop [pʰ], with a puff of air after the release. The first sound of “beer” is often an unvoiced, unaspirated stop [p]. In most varieties of French, the contrast is really between [p] in “pou” and [b] in “boue.”","sub":["Putting a hand in front of the mouth while saying peer and beer lets you feel the aspiration.","**Slashes** mark **broad transcription** (writing the phonemes of an utterance), as in /p/.","**Square brackets** mark **narrow transcription** (including as much detail as possible), as in [pʰ]."],"end":"So broad transcription records the categories that tell words apart, and narrow transcription records how each sound was actually produced."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p015","m1-lecture01-p016","m1-lecture01-p017"],"main":"**Vowels are described by tongue height, frontness and lip rounding, but real vowels are messier than one point on a chart.** Close vowels tend to have a higher jaw position than open vowels. Front vowels have the tongue body farther forward than back vowels. **Rounding** of the lips is a separate dimension.","sub":["/y/ is the vowel in French “tu” and Mandarin “qu.” Making an /i/ and then rounding the lips turns it toward /y/.","Some vowels, like the **diphthongs** in “boat” and “bait,” are very dynamic, because the tongue moves during the vowel.","Vowels also differ across dialects. In the mid-Atlantic dialect of New York City’s outer boroughs, CAUGHT and COT have different vowels. Most other Americans say them the same way."],"end":"So a vowel chart gives useful reference points, but each speaker’s vowel system has to be checked by listening to that speaker."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why do we need the IPA when languages already have alphabets?","a":"We need the IPA because letters are not phonemes, and each writing system uses its own spelling conventions. Even regular systems like Pinyin and French spelling cannot be read correctly with English letter habits. The IPA gives one standard symbol per sound, so sounds can be compared accurately across languages.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p007","m1-lecture01-p008"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What three features organize the IPA consonant chart?","a":"The chart is organized by place, manner and voicing. Place of articulation runs from the front of the vocal tract to the back, and manner runs from fully closed to open. Voicing separates the members of each pair by when the vocal folds start vibrating relative to the closure.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p009","m1-review-p004"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How are /p/ and /b/ alike, and how are they different?","a":"/p/ and /b/ are alike in manner and place, because both are bilabial plosives. They differ in voicing, which is the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure. The same difference separates /t/ from /d/, so we can describe a whole class of voiced stops /b/, /d/ and /g/.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p010","m1-review-p005"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the difference between /p/ and [pʰ]?","a":"/p/ is a broad transcription that names the phoneme, while [pʰ] is a narrow transcription of one specific pronunciation. Slashes mark the category a language uses to tell words apart. Square brackets record as much detail as possible, such as the aspiration in the first sound of “peer.”","refs":["m1-lecture01-p012","m1-lecture01-p011"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How do English and French differ in producing the voicing contrast for stops?","a":"English often contrasts an aspirated [pʰ] in “peer” with an unaspirated [p] in “beer,” so neither sound is fully voiced. Most varieties of French contrast [p] in “pou” with a truly voiced [b] in “boue.” So the same broad labels, voiced and voiceless, are produced in different ways in different languages.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p011"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-phonemes-allophones","title":"Phonemes, minimal pairs and allophones","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p019","m1-lecture01-p020"],"main":"**A minimal pair proves that two sounds are different phonemes in a language.** A **minimal pair** is two words whose difference in meaning depends on a difference in one sound. If two phones are **contrastive** in this way, they are examples of different phonemes. CAUGHT and COT are a minimal pair for a speaker who distinguishes the two low back vowels.","sub":["Minimal pairs also reveal which feature a contrast depends on.","In English, /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/ all differ on voicing. We know this by comparing minimal pairs in English itself."],"end":"So the evidence for a phoneme always comes from word contrasts in the language being studied."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p021","m1-lecture01-p022","m1-lecture01-p023"],"main":"**Allophones are different phones that count as the same phoneme because no minimal pair separates them.** Phonemes are realized in many ways, and some of this variation is very predictable. Recordings of “pot that,” “spot that” and “stop that” make it possible to compare how /p/ is released and aspirated in each position.","sub":["We sometimes say [stɑpʰ] with an aspirated release, often when stressing the word or speaking clearly in citation form.","There is no difference in meaning between [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ], and no minimal pair separates these two phones in these contexts.","So they are **allophones**, from allo meaning different, which gives “different phones for the same phoneme.”","The forms “stop,” “stops,” “stopped” and “stopping” put /p/ in new positions, so its release and aspiration change with the morphological form."],"end":"So hearing a difference between two sounds does not make them different phonemes, because only a meaning contrast does that."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p024"],"main":"**The word voicing is used in two different ways.** In narrow transcription of specific phones, a sound is called voiced if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole sound. In broad transcription of phonemes, voicing is a contrast that can be realized in several ways.","sub":["One realization is the presence or absence of aspiration at the beginnings of words.","Another is the relative duration of voicing to closure, as in “writer” and “rider.”"],"end":"So an English “voiced” stop can be a phoneme in the voiced category even when its vocal folds are not vibrating the whole time."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p025"],"main":"**Phonotactics are the rules for which phonemes can occur together in syllables.** English allows [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ] but never [sbɑroʊ], whatever the speaker thinks they are saying. When English speakers meet clusters their language forbids, they change them.","sub":["The Polish name Zbigniew becomes [zɨ’bIg.nʲu].","The name Gbagbo becomes [gə.bɑ.gə.boʊ], with vowels inserted to break up the clusters."],"end":"So the sound patterns of a language shape how its speakers produce even foreign words."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p026"],"main":"**A phoneme is the smallest phonetic unit in a language that is capable of conveying a distinction in meaning.** Phonemes are not letters. They are not even specific sounds or gestures, because one phoneme can have several allophones.","sub":["The IPA gives speech scientists a way to talk about similarities and differences between sounds across languages.","It is easy to think of the IPA as an “alphabet of phonemes,” but this is a mistake because phonemes are not letters."],"end":"So a phoneme is defined by the meaning contrasts it creates, not by a symbol or a single sound."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is a minimal pair, and what does it establish?","a":"A minimal pair is two words whose difference in meaning depends on a difference in a single sound. It establishes that the two sounds are contrastive, so they are different phonemes in that language. CAUGHT and COT do this for a speaker whose dialect keeps the two low back vowels apart.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p019","m1-review-p006"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why are [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ] allophones of /p/ rather than different phonemes?","a":"They are allophones because there is no difference in meaning between them. No minimal pair separates these two phones in these contexts in English. So they are different phones that realize the same phoneme /p/.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p023"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How do minimal pairs show that English stop contrasts depend on voicing?","a":"English has minimal pairs for /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/. In each pair the place and manner stay the same and only voicing changes. So voicing is the shared feature that carries the contrast, and this is known from comparing minimal pairs in English.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p020","m1-review-p007"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What are the two meanings of voicing?","a":"In narrow transcription, a phone is voiced if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole sound. In broad transcription, voicing is a phonemic contrast that can be realized in different ways. Those ways include aspiration at the beginnings of words and the duration of voicing relative to closure in “writer” and “rider.”","refs":["m1-lecture01-p024"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What are phonotactics, and how do they show up in speech?","a":"Phonotactics are the patterns that say which phonemes can occur together in syllables. English never allows [sbɑroʊ], so speakers say [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ]. Faced with names like Zbigniew or Gbagbo, English speakers insert vowels to fit English syllable patterns.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p025"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-speech-signal","title":"Speech is a continuous signal, not a row of letters","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p018"],"main":"**The lack of invariance problem is that one speech category has no single fixed acoustic form.** The same vowel sounds different across speakers and dialects. A listener still understands people who say “Aaron” and “Erin” with the same vowel.","sub":["The vowel measurements for each category spread widely and overlap with neighboring categories.","So a listener cannot identify a vowel by checking for one fixed set of acoustic values."],"end":"So understanding speech means interpreting variable sound as stable categories, and this problem comes up again and again."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p027","m1-lecture01-p028"],"main":"**Speech is a continuously changing signal, not an orderly stream of letter-like units.** Literate people easily imagine that speech is produced as separate letters. A spectrogram of “A sore back is a real pain” has no clean boundaries between the sounds.","sub":["The lips, jaw and tongue move continuously during speech.","Each movement overlaps with the movements for neighboring sounds, so the acoustic signal blends them together."],"end":"So phonemes are useful categories, but they are not separate pieces lined up in the signal."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p029"],"main":"**Hockett (1955) compared speech to a row of raw eggs smashed through a wringer.** The eggs on the belt stand for the series of phonemes the speaker intends. The wringer smashes and mixes them, and that mess stands for the speech signal. An inspector studies the mess and decides which eggs went in.","sub":["The inspector represents the hearer.","He does not have to put the eggs back together, which is physically impossible, but only to identify them."],"end":"So the listener’s job is to identify the intended phonemes from an overlapping signal, not to recover separate pieces."},{"refs":["m1-lecture01-p030"],"main":"**Some researchers have started to question whether phonemes are psychologically or linguistically real.** Phonemes are a useful way to describe contrasts. It is a separate question whether speakers actually store and recognize words in phoneme-sized units.","end":"So the phoneme is a strong descriptive tool whose status in the mind is still debated."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is it so important that phonemes are not letters?","a":"Phonemes are not letters because speech is a continuously changing signal, not an orderly stream of letter-like units. A spectrogram of “A sore back is a real pain” has no clean breaks between sounds. Neighboring sounds overlap, so each phoneme looks different depending on its context.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p027","m1-review-p008"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the lack of invariance problem?","a":"The lack of invariance problem is that a single speech category has no fixed acoustic form. The same vowel varies across speakers and dialects, and the categories overlap. Listeners still understand speech, so they must map variable sounds onto stable categories.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p018"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Explain Hockett’s egg analogy and what each part stands for.","a":"Hockett (1955) imagined raw eggs on a belt being smashed together by a wringer. The eggs stand for the intended phonemes, and the smashed mess stands for the speech signal. The inspector who identifies the eggs from the mess stands for the hearer, who only has to identify the phonemes, not rebuild them.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p029","m1-review-p009"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m1-morphology","title":"Morphemes, derivation and inflection","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p004","m1-lecture03-p005"],"main":"**Words are built from meaningful parts called morphemes, and “knifes” shows two kinds of morphology at once.** In a Scrabble argument, one player says the plural of knife is knives. The other answers that Tony “knifes” Bernardo in West Side Story. Both are right, because knife the noun and knife the verb are different words.","sub":["**Derivational morphology** derives new words from existing parts, and **zero derivation** turns the noun knife into a verb with no added ending.","**Inflectional morphology** then adds -s, because the sentence “He knifes him” needs a third person singular verb.","Morphology itself is morph- (form) plus -ology (the study of), which gives “the study of the forms of words.”"],"end":"So a word form can only be judged by its grammatical role in the sentence."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p006","m1-lecture03-p007","m1-lecture03-p008"],"main":"**Derivational morphology is productive, but in limited ways, so the meaning of a derived word is only partly predictable.** Even zero derivation usually works with a narrow sense of the base word. To “knife” somebody uses one particular kind of knife, not a butter knife.","sub":["The suffix -hood means a state of being, as in brotherhood and sisterhood.","Knighthood is odd, because two brothers share their brotherhood but each knight has his own knighthood.","Neighborhood is a good word, but -hood does a different job in it, and we say friendship rather than friendhood for no clear reason.","Strong associates of knife become verbs with unrelated meanings, as in “dish it out,” “a forked tongue” and “table the issue.”"],"end":"So derivation creates new words, but which words exist and what they mean is partly a matter of convention."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p009"],"main":"**Derivation often changes the pronunciation of the rest of the word, not only adding an ending.** Certificate /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/ becomes certification /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/ and then recertification /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/. The stress moves and the vowels change as affixes are added.","sub":["The noun and verb forms of record and invite differ mainly in stress.","This is one reason for “The Chaos” of English spelling. It is also why spelling closer to the spoken form would not necessarily improve readability."],"end":"So adding an affix can reshape the sound of the whole word."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p010","m1-lecture03-p011","m1-lecture03-p012"],"main":"**Inflectional morphology changes a word’s grammatical form in a predictable way and is required by the syntax.** English inflection marks plurals, as in knife and knives or one mouse and a lot of mice. It also marks third person singular present (she walks) and past tense (I walked, she sang). Menn notes that it would be silly to have separate dictionary entries for singular and plural nouns.","sub":["The sound change can be irregular, as in mice and sang, even though the grammatical meaning stays predictable.","In the phrase “two [noun]+pl,” whatever noun goes there must be pluralized somehow.","No derivational form is required by syntax in this way, and a formula like “Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it” is not syntax.","English uses little inflection compared with many languages. Others mark case on nouns, form plurals differently for small and large numbers, or conjugate verbs more elaborately."],"end":"So inflection keeps the same word but fits it to its grammatical job in the sentence."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p013"],"main":"**Derivation and inflection both build new forms from old parts, but they do different jobs.** Both create new phonological forms and change the meanings of base forms. **Derivation** creates new words and is productive only in limited ways. **Inflection** changes how words interact syntactically, is highly productive but not always regular, and is required by syntax.","end":"So the test is whether the change makes a new word or fits the same word into a sentence."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why is “knifes” a word, even though the plural of knife is knives?","a":"“Knifes” is a word because it is the verb knife with third person singular -s, as in “He knifes him.” Knives is the plural of the noun knife. Zero derivation made knife into a verb, and inflection then added the ending the sentence requires.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p004"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is a morpheme, and how does the word morphology itself break into morphemes?","a":"A morpheme is a meaningful part that words are built from. Morphology is morph-, meaning form, plus -ology, meaning the study of. Together they give “the study of the forms of words.”","refs":["m1-lecture03-p005","m1-review-p011"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"In what ways is derivational morphology productive but limited?","a":"Derivation can make new words, but the meaning of the result is only partly predictable and affixes do not apply to every base. The suffix -hood works for brotherhood and sisterhood but does a different job in neighborhood. We say friendship instead of friendhood for no clear reason.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p007","m1-review-p012","m1-lecture03-p006"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How can derivation change the pronunciation of a word?","a":"Adding an affix can move the stress and change the vowels in the rest of the word. Certificate, certification and recertification each have a different stress pattern. Record and invite change stress between their noun and verb forms.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p009","m1-review-p014"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What makes inflection different from derivation?","a":"Inflection changes how a word works in the sentence, while derivation creates a new word. Inflection is highly productive and required by syntax, as when “two” forces the next noun to be plural. Derivation is productive only in limited ways, and no sentence ever requires a particular derived form.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p013","m1-lecture03-p011","m1-review-p016"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m1-phrases-roles","title":"Phrases, clauses and semantic roles","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p014","m1-lecture03-p015","m1-lecture03-p016"],"main":"**A noun phrase has an abstract structure that very different word strings can share.** Nouns, verbs, adjectives and adverbs are **content words**. **Function words** like determiners and prepositions organize them into phrases. “The video for ‘More than Words’” is a noun phrase with the structure Det. + N + PP.","sub":["The is a determiner that begins the noun phrase.","For is a preposition that begins a prepositional phrase, and that phrase specifies which video is meant.","“Two guys from the early ‘nineties” and “a band with only one hit” share the Det. + N + PP structure."],"end":"So phrase structure describes relations among categories, not particular words."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p017"],"main":"**Abstract categories make it possible to compare languages that build phrases differently.** All languages have noun phrases, but they form them very differently. The Chinese phrase 咖啡店裡面的小貓 goes PP + N, with “coffeeshop inside” before “cat.”","sub":["The Chinese noun phrase has no determiner.","This helps explain why determiners are so difficult for speakers of Chinese languages to learn in a second language."],"end":"So without abstraction we could not talk about very different surface forms as similar."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p018","m1-lecture03-p019"],"main":"**Prepositional phrases are P + NP, and phrases sit inside other phrases.** “From the early ‘nineties” is a preposition followed by a noun phrase. That noun phrase has its own structure, Det + Adj. + N.","sub":["The determiner, adjective and noun are inside the noun phrase.","The noun phrase is inside the prepositional phrase, which will be inside a sentence or another noun phrase.","This nesting is why people started using trees to diagram sentences."],"end":"So sentence structure is hierarchical, not just a string of words in a row."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p020","m1-lecture03-p021","m1-lecture03-p022"],"main":"**A clause is a verb phrase and its subject, and the subject is the noun phrase that controls the verb.** Verb phrases contain different complements, such as V + NP in “sang the song.” Another is V + NP + PP in “threw a party for him.” In the example sentence, the whole first noun phrase is the subject and its head noun is “video.”","sub":["In “The plums that were in the icebox were delicious,” the verb agrees with “plums,” not “icebox.” This is because plums is the head of the noun phrase.","Adding “which you were probably saving for breakfast” puts a clause inside a clause.","Changing “you” to “my roommate” changes only the inner verb, which becomes “was saving.”"],"end":"So agreement reveals which noun phrase controls which verb."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p023","m1-lecture03-p024"],"main":"**Syntactic roles and semantic roles are different, so the subject is not always the agent.** “I have eaten the plums in the icebox” and “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me” mean the same thing. In the passive sentence, the plums are the subject, because the verb “were eaten” agrees with the plural noun.","sub":["“By me” is just a prepositional phrase fleshing out the verb phrase.","**Semantic roles** describe who did what to whom, and here they are Undergoer (the plums), Location (the icebox) and **Agent** (me).","These semantic roles stay the same in both sentences."],"end":"So “me” feels like the subject only because English usually puts the agent in the subject position."},{"refs":["m1-lecture03-p025"],"main":"**Language is highly generative, and we do most of it without thinking.** We make new words with derivational morphology. We organize words into phrases, clauses and sentences with syntax. We modify words for their syntactic roles with inflectional morphology.","end":"So some of the most complex things linguistics must explain are hiding in plain sight."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is the structure of “The video for ‘More than Words,’” and what job do its function words do?","a":"It is a noun phrase with the structure Det. + N + PP. The determiner “the” begins the noun phrase, and the preposition “for” begins a prepositional phrase that tells which video is meant. The same structure fits phrases with completely different words, such as “a band with only one hit.”","refs":["m1-lecture03-p015","m1-lecture03-p016"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"In “The plums that were in the icebox were delicious,” why is the verb “were” and not “was”?","a":"The verb is “were” because it agrees with “plums,” the head of the subject noun phrase. “Icebox” is singular, but it sits inside a modifying phrase and does not control the verb. So agreement follows the head of the subject, not the nearest noun.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p022"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"In “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me,” why is “me” not the subject?","a":"“Me” is not the subject because the verb “were eaten” agrees with the plural noun plums, which makes the plums the subject. “By me” is a prepositional phrase inside the verb phrase. “Me” is still the agent, because semantic roles do not change between active and passive sentences.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p023","m1-lecture03-p024"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why do we need abstract categories like NP to compare languages?","a":"We need them because languages build the same kind of phrase in very different ways. English says “the cat in the coffee shop,” while Chinese puts the location before the noun and has no determiner. Only an abstract category like NP lets us call both noun phrases and describe how they differ.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p017"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-activation","title":"Activation, word recognition and priming","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p002","m1-lecture04-p003","m1-lecture04-p004","m1-lecture04-p005","m1-lecture04-p006","m1-lecture04-p007","m1-lecture04-p008","m1-lecture04-p009","m1-lecture04-p010","m1-lecture04-p012","m1-lecture04-p013"],"main":"**Association norms record the first words people write down when they see a cue word.** Participants saw cues such as LION, TRIPE, FREE, STRIPES, PEAR, BEAR, TREE and TIGER for a few seconds. Three items, LION, TIGER and STRIPES, matter later.","sub":["Pairs like TRIPE and STRIPES, or PEAR and BEAR, look alike but mean unrelated things, so the relation between form and meaning is arbitrary.","Associated is different from similar, because TIGER and STRIPES are associated even though a tiger and a stripe are not similar things.","The list itself can shape the answers, so a list of only wild animals or only household appliances could produce different associates."],"end":"So association norms measure which words bring each other to mind under particular task conditions."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p014","m1-lecture04-p015","m1-lecture04-p016"],"main":"**Psycholinguistics borrows its basic ideas from neuroscience, starting with activation.** These ideas are activation, spreading of activation over weighted connections, distributed representations, associative learning, and top-down and bottom-up processing. They are the basis for most theory and experimental design in the field.","sub":["**Hubel and Weisel (1968)** recorded simple cells in primary visual cortex (V1) that respond to lines of particular orientations in a particular receptive field.","One cell activates most strongly to upward movement of a line at about 315°, so it represents that combination of features.","A cell further up the visual processing stream responds to something more complex, like the profile of another monkey."],"end":"So “activation” in psycholinguistics extends this idea, as a metaphor, to perceptual and conceptual representations."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p017","m1-lecture04-p018","m1-lecture04-p019","m1-lecture04-p020","m1-lecture04-p021"],"main":"**A word can be modeled as a node whose activation rises with the strength of the evidence for it.** Imagine a simple network with nodes for LION, TIGER and STRIPES. Each node can be activated by its word, in any case, font or size, so recognition faces the same lack of invariance problem as phonemes.","sub":["Activation can be partial, so when LION contrasts poorly with a noise mask, its node is only weakly activated.","As the stimulus becomes clearer, activation increases.","The **signal-to-noise ratio** is how strong the word is compared with the background noise. When it is very favorable, activity is strong enough that the network has identified “lion.”","This is the **perceptual identification task**, where participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item."],"end":"So identification time measures how quickly a word’s activation reaches the point of recognition."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p022","m1-lecture04-p023"],"main":"**How quickly a word is identified depends on the stimulus itself and on the experimental environment.** Features of the stimulus include how frequently the word is encountered and how similar it is to other words. Features of the environment include whether you just saw the same word and whether you just saw a related word.","sub":["Seeing the same word again speeds identification, and this is **repetition priming**.","Seeing a similar or related word first can also change identification speed."],"end":"So a fast identification could come from the word’s own history or from what happened just before it."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p024","m1-review-p021"],"main":"**Frequency effects can be explained by experience tuning up a word’s activation dynamics.** Most models of this kind assume that activation unfolds over time. Words encountered more often are tuned to be more sensitive, so they are easier to activate.","sub":["Every encounter with a word might raise its **baseline** (resting) level of activation.","It might strengthen the word’s incoming connections, so the word is activated more quickly.","It might change a **threshold** parameter, so less activation is needed to recognize the word.","Rumelhart and McClelland’s 1986 Interactive Activation model, with its plot of CAVE rising above competing words, is one model that simulates frequency effects."],"end":"So the frequency effect is a very simple form of learning built into how activation rises."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p025"],"main":"**Repetition priming happens because activation falls off more slowly than it rises.** Activation does not rise and fall instantaneously. If a word is seen again while its activation is still high from the last exposure, its starting point is already higher than usual.","sub":["So the word needs less new input to reach recognition.","This is repetition priming, a basic effect in psychology."],"end":"So leftover activation from the first exposure gives the second exposure a head start."}],"questions":[{"q":"How is “associated” different from “similar”?","a":"Two words are associated when one tends to bring the other to mind, and similar when they share properties. TIGER and STRIPES are associated, but a tiger and a stripe are not similar things. LION and TIGER are both similar animals and associated words.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p013","m1-lecture04-p012"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Describe how a simple lexical network identifies the word LION in the perceptual identification task.","a":"The LION node is weakly activated when the word contrasts poorly with a noise mask. As the word becomes clearer, its activation increases. When the signal-to-noise ratio is very favorable, activation is strong enough to count as identification, and the participant pushes the button.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p019","m1-lecture04-p020","m1-lecture04-p021","m1-review-p020"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Give three ways experience could produce a frequency effect in an activation model.","a":"Each encounter with a word might raise its baseline level of activation, so it starts closer to recognition. It might strengthen the incoming connections, so the word is activated more quickly. It might change a threshold parameter, so less activation is needed for recognition.","refs":["m1-review-p021","m1-lecture04-p024"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why does seeing a word again shortly afterward make it faster to identify?","a":"It is faster because activation falls off more slowly than it rises. When the word appears again, some activation from the first exposure is still there, so its baseline is higher than usual. The word needs less new input to be identified, and this is repetition priming.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p025","m1-review-p022"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Hubel and Weisel (1968) find, and why does it matter for psycholinguistics?","a":"Hubel and Weisel (1968) found simple cells in primary visual cortex that respond to lines of particular orientations in a particular receptive field. One cell responds most strongly to upward movement of a line at about 315°, so it represents that combination of features. Psycholinguistics extends this idea of activation to perceptual and conceptual representations.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p015","m1-lecture04-p016"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-spreading-activation","title":"Spreading activation, mediated priming and learning","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p026","m1-lecture04-p027","m1-lecture04-p028"],"main":"**Spreading activation explains how one word can prime a different, related word.** Two effects need more than single nodes. These are **neighborhood effects** (effects of how similar a word is to other words) and associative, semantic or phonological priming. Both require the nodes in the network to be connected.","sub":["Connection strengths between nodes are set by their association, as measured in the norms.","So when “lion” is activated, some of that activation travels along a weighted connection to “tiger.”","This is the spreading activation account of **semantic priming** (faster recognition of a word after a related word)."],"end":"So a related prime gives the target a head start before the target even appears."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p040","m1-lecture04-p030","m1-lecture04-p031","m1-lecture04-p032","m1-lecture04-p033","m1-lecture04-p034","m1-lecture04-p035","m1-lecture04-p036","m1-lecture04-p037","m1-lecture04-p038","m1-lecture04-p039","m1-lecture04-p041"],"main":"**Mediated priming is priming between two words that are linked only through a third word.** STRIPES and LION are not directly associated. STRIPES is linked to TIGER, and TIGER is linked to LION. Because activity flows throughout the network, activating “stripes” can still prime “lion.”","sub":["In the demonstration, STRIPES and then LION emerge gradually from a noise mask, as in a perceptual identification trial.","This kind of finding is very representative of psycholinguistics, with careful study design and small effects.","The result is counterintuitive, because there is no obvious reason stripes should prime lion except through tiger."],"end":"So mediated priming is compelling evidence that activation really spreads through a network."},{"refs":["m1-review-p025","m1-review-p027"],"main":"**Balota and Lorch found mediated priming when people read the target aloud, but not in lexical decision.** Their prime and target, such as lion and stripes, were related only through a mediating concept, tiger. In **lexical decision** (judging whether a string is a word), directly related pairs like lion and tiger were faster, but mediated pairs were not.","sub":["In **pronunciation** (reading the target aloud), both directly related and mediated pairs were faster than neutral or unrelated primes.","The mediated benefit was small but appeared at both prime-to-target delays.","They concluded that activation spreads beyond directly related concepts, and that features of the lexical decision task masked the mediated effect."],"end":"So whether a small priming effect appears can depend on the task used to measure it."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p042"],"main":"**Bottom-up processing builds words from features, and top-down processing lets word knowledge shape how letters are seen.** In the Interactive Activation network, feature detectors activate letter nodes, and letter nodes activate word nodes such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK. Activation for a word then feeds back down to the letters that fit it.","sub":["When the last letter of WORK is partly hidden, activation for WORK changes how that letter is interpreted.","This top-down influence is one way of modeling **pattern completion**, which Menn discusses at length."],"end":"So recognition combines evidence from the input with expectations from stored words."},{"refs":["m1-lecture04-p043","m1-lecture04-p044","m1-lecture04-p045"],"main":"**Associative learning explains how these networks get into the brain.** The simple, powerful idea is that “neurons that fire together, wire together.” This is **Hebbian learning**, in which units active at the same time strengthen their connection.","sub":["These mechanisms were historically considered insufficient to learn anything important about language.","More recent arguments are about what role they play and how innate knowledge constrains them. This matters because large language models learn many structural features of language this way.","To generalize, an associative model must represent similarity, so it uses **distributed representations** (patterns of activity across many units).","So what you know about robins and canaries can generalize to new birds as you learn about them."],"end":"So activation, spreading activation, top-down processing, associative learning and distributed representations together form the toolkit for modeling lexical networks."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why do neighborhood effects and related-word priming require adding connections to a network?","a":"They require connections because they involve one word changing the activity of a different word. A single isolated node can only explain changes in its own activity, as in repetition priming. Weighted connections let activation travel from a prime like lion to a related word like tiger.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p026","m1-review-p023"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is mediated priming, and why is it strong evidence for spreading activation?","a":"Mediated priming is priming between two words that are linked only through a third word, such as lion and stripes through tiger. Lion and stripes are not directly associated, so a direct association cannot explain the effect. The effect only makes sense if activation spreads onward through the network.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p040","m1-review-p026"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Balota and Lorch find in lexical decision and in pronunciation?","a":"In lexical decision, directly related primes helped but mediated primes did not. In pronunciation, both directly related and mediated primes helped compared with neutral and unrelated primes. They concluded that activation does spread beyond directly related concepts, and that the lexical decision task masked the mediated effect.","refs":["m1-review-p025","m1-review-p027"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What is the difference between bottom-up and top-down processing in the Interactive Activation model?","a":"Bottom-up processing sends activation from visual features to letters and then to words. Top-down processing sends activation from a word like WORK back down to the letters that fit it. This feedback helps complete a partly hidden letter, which is a model of pattern completion.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p042"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why do associative models need distributed representations?","a":"They need them because an associative model must represent similarity in order to generalize. In a distributed representation, similar items share parts of their activity pattern. So what is learned about robins and canaries can carry over to a new bird.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p044"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-localization","title":"Localization of language, from phrenology to Lichtheim’s house","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p002","m1-lecture05-p003"],"main":"**Phrenology was an early attempt to relate brain structure to function by measuring skulls.** It rested on three premises. Different parts of the brain do different processes, aptitude is related to the size of brain regions, and region size shapes the skull.","sub":["Its first problem was **inappropriate anatomical measurement**, because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape.","Its second problem was an **unconstrained definition of functions**. Researchers studied prisoners to locate “criminality,” and women with different numbers of children to locate “philopropogenitiveness.”"],"end":"So phrenology failed on both the anatomy and the definition of the functions, even though its first premise is not obviously wrong."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p004"],"main":"**Flourens used experimental lesions to argue against phrenology.** Working mainly with pigeons and rabbits, he established distinct functions for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and midbrain structures. He failed to find any match between specific cortical regions and specific senses.","sub":["He insisted that the size of the lesion was what mattered.","When most of the cortex was removed, sight, hearing and the other senses disappeared together."],"end":"So Flourens argued that the cortex worked as a whole rather than as a map of separate sense centers."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p005","m1-lecture05-p006"],"main":"**In 1861, Broca presented the case of “Tan,” who had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier.** Tan could only produce one jargon syllable. He otherwise seemed to be “in possession of his senses,” and he seemed to understand what was said.","sub":["Tan’s brain had a large lesion that definitely included the third convolution of the frontal lobe, the **inferior frontal gyrus**.","This gave us **Broca’s area**, a portion of the left frontal lobe that is important for speech.","Hughlings Jackson (1874) objected that “to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things.”"],"end":"So Tan linked a specific injury to a specific loss, but the damaged site is not necessarily where speech itself is located."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p007"],"main":"**Lichtheim’s house model explains different aphasias as damage to centers or to the pathways between them.** In the 1870s, German neurologists including Lichtheim and Wernicke made more systematic observations of aphasia. They ascribed specific losses of function to “disconnections” between “centers.”","sub":["Sound comes in along the acoustic pathway, a, to **A**, the center for sound images.","**A** connects up to **B**, the center for concepts, so heard words can be understood.","**B** connects down to **M**, the center for movement images, so ideas can be spoken.","**M** sends commands out along the motor pathway, m, and a direct path from A to M allows verbatim repetition.","So a disconnection between A and B could cause a loss of understanding of spoken words. A disconnection between A and M could cause a loss of repetition."],"end":"So each numbered break in the house predicts a different form of aphasia, and this center-based approach is an ancestor of modularity."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p008"],"main":"**Freud criticized the diagram-drawing school as naïve.** In his early neurological research, he doubted that the brain is organized by psychological categories like “word forms” or “concepts.” He proposed instead that language emerged from “fields” connecting perceptual and motor abilities to “drives.”","end":"So Freud raised a lasting question in cognitive neuroscience, whether brain areas really match our descriptions of cognitive functions."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p009","m1-lecture05-p010","m1-lecture05-p011"],"main":"**MRI of Broca’s original brains revealed that the damage was much deeper and wider than Broca’s area.** Dronkers and colleagues found that Leborgne’s lesion was very deep and included the **insula**. There was also heavy white matter damage to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus**, which connects the front and back of the cerebral cortex.","sub":["Other work by this group suggests that the insula, not Broca’s area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**, the condition Broca described.","Broca’s behavioral description was crude, because linguistics then focused on history and experimental psychology had not been invented.","His anatomy was also crude and probably wrong, because detailed cortical maps came later, and Brodmann’s map appeared only in 1909.","Others such as the Daxes and Wernicke showed similar things, and Wernicke’s fluent patient produced meaningless gibberish."],"end":"So we still talk about Broca because his case made relating specific functions to specific brain regions a respectable pursuit."}],"questions":[{"q":"What were phrenology’s premises, and which ones were wrong?","a":"Phrenology assumed that brain parts do different processes, that aptitude depends on region size, and that region size shapes the skull. The idea that different brain parts do different things is not obviously wrong. The method failed because skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape and the functions, like “criminality,” were not properly defined.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p002","m1-lecture05-p003"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Broca observe in Tan, and what did Hughlings Jackson object?","a":"Broca observed that Tan could produce only one jargon syllable but seemed to understand what was said. Tan’s brain had a large lesion including the left inferior frontal gyrus, which became Broca’s area. Hughlings Jackson (1874) objected that locating the damage that destroys speech is not the same as locating speech.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p005","m1-lecture05-p006","m1-review-p030","m1-review-p031"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Explain Lichtheim’s house model and two disconnections it predicts.","a":"Lichtheim’s house has a center for sound images (A), a center for concepts (B) and a center for movement images (M), joined by pathways. A disconnection between A and B predicts a loss of understanding of spoken words. A disconnection between A and M predicts a loss of verbatim repetition.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p007","m1-review-p032"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What was Freud’s critique of the aphasia diagram-makers?","a":"Freud argued that it was naïve to expect the brain to be organized by psychological categories like word forms or concepts. He proposed instead that language came from fields connecting perceptual and motor abilities to drives. His critique raises the lasting question of whether brain areas match cognitive functions.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p008","m1-review-p033"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did Dronkers and colleagues find when they scanned Broca’s cases?","a":"They found that the lesion was very deep, including the insula, with heavy white matter damage to the superior longitudinal fasciculus. Their other work links speech apraxia, the condition Broca described, to the insula rather than Broca’s area. So Broca’s original localization was probably wrong.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p009","m1-review-p034"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]},{"id":"m1-brain-methods","title":"Lesion studies, neuroimaging and subtraction logic","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p013","m1-lecture05-p014"],"main":"**Cognitive neuropsychology depends on cognitive psychology to define the functions it tests.** Much of what we learned about structure and function in the twentieth century came from experiments with brain-damaged individuals. Theory supplies hypotheses about which operations might be separable, such as recognizing the abstract forms of words and computing their meaning.","sub":["Quantitative measurements allow statistical comparison, which is critical because deficits are rarely complete.","Anatomical imaging, such as the scan of H.M.’s middle temporal lobes, locates injuries without waiting for autopsy.","But studies of brain-injured patients show what behavior looks like after damage, so they tell us only indirectly what the intact region does."],"end":"So Hughlings Jackson’s warning still applies even with modern scans."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p015","m1-lecture05-p016"],"main":"**Metabolic imaging such as PET and fMRI studies brains in action, but it measures an indirect index.** The PET studies described by Posner and colleagues used radio-labeled oxygen as a marker for activity. These methods track oxygen consumption, not neural activity itself.","sub":["They are slow, because metabolic processes unfold over seconds while important neural events take milliseconds or less.","They have only fair spatial resolution, in millimeters, while important events happen at the micron scale.","There is activity all over the brain all the time, so a task must be compared with a **control condition**.","This **subtraction logic** takes the task image minus the control image to isolate the operation that differs between them."],"end":"So an activation map is only meaningful relative to a carefully chosen control."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p017","m1-lecture05-p018","m1-lecture05-p019"],"main":"**Posner and colleagues used subtraction to localize semantic processing of words, and the region roughly matched Broca’s area.** They compared monitoring visual words for dangerous animals with passive viewing. They also compared generating uses for words with simply repeating them, for visual and for auditory words.","sub":["All three semantic contrasts activated the left inferior prefrontal cortex.","They concluded that the PET data support localizing operations on visual, phonological and semantic codes.","Whole tasks like word reading or shifting attention are not performed by any single brain area, because each involves many component computations."],"end":"So the answer to “is this better than phrenology?” rests on defining component operations carefully and testing them with controlled contrasts."},{"refs":["m1-lecture05-p020"],"main":"**Language has turned up at key moments in debates about localization of function.** Broca’s presentation of Tan helped establish **lesion-behavior analysis**. Some of the very first PET and fMRI studies used activation of Broca’s area in language tasks as evidence that cognitive functions could be localized.","end":"So language is well suited to these studies because it breaks into distinct abilities, like speaking, understanding and repeating, that can be measured separately."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why does cognitive neuropsychology depend on cognitive psychology?","a":"It depends on cognitive psychology for definitions of the functions being tested. Theory says which operations might be separable, such as recognizing word forms and computing meaning. Quantitative measures then let those operations be compared statistically, which matters because deficits are rarely complete.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p013"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What are three limits of metabolic imaging like PET and fMRI?","a":"First, they are metabolic, so they measure an index of oxygen consumption rather than neural activity. Second, they are slow, because metabolic changes take seconds while neural events take milliseconds. Third, their spatial resolution is in millimeters, while important events happen at the micron scale.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p015"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How did Posner and colleagues use subtraction logic to isolate semantic processing?","a":"They subtracted a control task from a semantic task that differed only in needing word meaning. Monitoring words for dangerous animals was compared with passive viewing, and generating uses was compared with repeating words. The left inferior frontal region active in all three semantic contrasts corresponded roughly to Broca’s area.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p016","m1-lecture05-p017"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Is PET localization better than phrenology?","a":"It is better because it localizes carefully defined component operations, not broad traits, and it uses controlled task contrasts. Posner and colleagues argued that operations on visual, phonological and semantic codes are localized. They also argued that whole tasks like reading are not done by any single area.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p018","m1-lecture05-p019"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-cognitive-revolution","title":"Behaviorism, AI and Chomsky’s Cognitive Revolution","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p002","m1-lecture06-p003"],"main":"**The Cognitive Revolution brought back the idea of language as a formal, symbolic system.** Experimental psychology in the 1950s and 1960s was actively hostile to talking about internal states. **Skinner’s** Verbal Behavior tried to explain how language is learned by **operant conditioning**. **Chomsky’s (1959)** review of the book is better remembered than the book itself.","sub":["The formal tradition connects to artificial intelligence, which dates back to the beginning of the digital computer.","Computers were the first machines that did so many kinds of calculations and logical operations, so people asked how they differed from human intelligence."],"end":"So the revolution made internal rules and representations legitimate parts of a theory of language."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p004","m1-lecture06-p005"],"main":"**The Turing test asks whether a computer can replace a person in a conversation game without the examiner noticing.** In the imitation game, A and B are a man and a woman, and C is an examiner who must figure out their genders. A tries to fool C, and B tries to help C.","sub":["If a computer replaces A or B and C does not notice, the computer has shown “intelligence.”","The test depends on natural language and on deception.","Turing explicitly excluded expert behaviors like calculating large sums or composing poetry.","He concluded that the best route to an intelligent machine is to build one with a child’s capabilities. It would then learn to be more like an adult."],"end":"So Turing tied intelligence to ordinary conversation and learning rather than to expert skills."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p006","m1-lecture06-p007"],"main":"**Early AI, called GOFAI, solved formal problems by applying procedures to symbols.** Newell and Simon’s **General Problem Solver** took an abstract description of a problem and applied procedures to symbols to find a solution. It was very good at games and puzzles like the Tower of Hanoi and chess, and at geometry proofs and algebra word problems.","sub":["Technology favored formal problems, because input was limited to punchcards and machine language. There was no way to give a computer an image and ask it to find the face.","Theory also favored them, because rational behavior was taken as the model of intelligence.","A trained pigeon can guide a missile without knowing what it is doing. AI programs at least represent goal states and choose procedures to reach them."],"end":"So early AI treated intelligence as rational symbol manipulation."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p008","m1-lecture06-p009","m1-lecture06-p010","m1-lecture06-p011"],"main":"**Chomsky argued that a speaker’s knowledge is an internalized grammar, a set of abstract rules that produce grammatical sentences.** There are an infinite number of grammatical sentences. We can still judge whether any new sentence is grammatical, which is remarkable. So people’s **grammaticality judgments** became the key data for the study of language.","sub":["In behaviorism, **stimulus generalization** spreads a response to similar stimuli. A pigeon trained to peck for a green light also pecks for similar colors.","That kind of generalization depends on concrete similarity, such as color or number.","Grammatical sentences have no obvious similarity to one another, so generalization cannot explain how we recognize new ones.","Chomsky added that all normal children acquire comparably complex grammars remarkably fast, which suggests humans are specially designed to do this."],"end":"So we recognize a new sentence because our grammar generates it, not because it resembles sentences we have heard."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p012","m1-lecture06-p013","m1-lecture06-p014"],"main":"**Chomsky attacked stimulus control, the idea that the environment determines what a speaker says.** For Skinner, saying “Mozart” in response to a piece of music is an example of stimulus control. Chomsky objected that “we do not know what the current stimuli are until he responds.”","sub":["So Skinner was posing the wrong question. Why people say what they do depends on social interactions, cognitive processes and past experience, much of it unrelated to language.","The tractable problem is to study the structure of what people say and derive the rules that produce it.","Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” had ever been heard, yet only the first is grammatical (Chomsky, 1955).","“Car! Getting towed! Yours!” is ungrammatical even though the stimulus that controlled it is perfectly clear."],"end":"So grammaticality depends on structure, not on past experience or on the stimulus."}],"questions":[{"q":"What is stimulus control, and why did Chomsky reject it as an explanation of language?","a":"Stimulus control is Skinner’s idea that an aspect of the environment controls a verbal response, like music prompting “Mozart.” Chomsky objected that we cannot know what the controlling stimulus was until the person responds. So the account cannot predict anything, and he argued the real question is the structure of what people say.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p012","m1-lecture06-p013"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why can’t stimulus generalization explain how we recognize new sentences?","a":"Stimulus generalization depends on concrete similarity, such as a pigeon pecking for colors close to green. Grammatical sentences have no obvious similarity to one another. So there is no dimension along which a new sentence resembles old ones, and Chomsky argued that an internalized grammar generates it instead.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p010","m1-lecture06-p011"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What does “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” tell us about grammar?","a":"It tells us that grammaticality does not depend on meaning or past experience. Neither that sentence nor its reversed version had been heard before, yet only the original is grammatical. “Car! Getting towed! Yours!” is clear in context but ungrammatical, so a clear stimulus does not make a sentence grammatical.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p014"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Describe the Turing test and its notable features.","a":"In the imitation game, an examiner tries to tell the genders of two hidden players, one of whom tries to fool him. A computer counts as intelligent if it replaces a player without the examiner noticing. The test relies on natural language and deception and excludes expert skills, and Turing suggested building a child-like machine that learns.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p004","m1-lecture06-p005"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why did early AI focus on formal problems?","a":"It focused on them partly because of technology, since input was limited to punchcards and machine language. It also focused on them for theoretical reasons, because rational behavior was seen as the model of intelligence. Programs like the General Problem Solver represented goals and chose procedures to reach them.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p007","m1-lecture06-p006"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-marr-emergentism","title":"Marr’s levels and the emergentist alternative","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p015","m1-lecture06-p016"],"main":"**Marr described cognitive systems at three levels of analysis, computational, algorithmic and implementational.** Chomsky was describing language at the **computational level**. A cash register makes the levels concrete.","sub":["At the computational level, a cash register takes prices and determines the amount due, then takes a payment and computes the change.","At the algorithmic level, it uses addition for the price and subtraction for the change.","At the implementational level, the hardware does not matter, so a credit card reader or a computer program are also “cash registers.”"],"end":"So the levels separate what a system computes, how it computes it, and what physically does the work."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p017","m1-lecture06-p018","m1-lecture06-p019","m1-lecture06-p020"],"main":"**Treating language as a Type I problem means studying grammar without worrying about how the brain implements it.** Marr called problems that break down cleanly into his levels “Type I” problems. For language, this means working at the computational level of Universal Grammar and the algorithmic level of specific processes.","sub":["The implementational level, with errors, memory limitations and associative learning, is set aside.","Marr equated Chomsky’s “competence” theory with a computational theory.","In a **Type 2** problem, many processes act at once, and their interaction is its own simplest description.","If language is a Type 2 problem, details about how it is implemented in the brain might be unavoidable in a theory of language."],"end":"So a whole alternative approach to language research is built on the idea that implementation matters."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p021","m1-lecture06-p022","m1-lecture06-p023","m1-lecture06-p024"],"main":"**Emergentist and parallel distributed processing approaches make their strong commitments at the implementational level.** They use **distributed representations**, like activity patterns over groups of neurons. They assume **gradual learning**, like adjusting the strength of synaptic connections.","sub":["**Elman (1990)** built **simple recurrent networks** whose only job is to predict the next item they will see, and they learn this gradually.","A letter-prediction network found the words in an unspaced text like “manyyearsagoaboyandgirl,” because its prediction error goes down within words.","Trained on simple sentences, a similar network grouped words into grammatical classes and even some semantic categories, as a side effect of learning to predict."],"end":"So word boundaries and word classes can emerge from prediction without being built in as rules."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p025"],"main":"**Statistical models trained on large corpora can tell “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” from its reversal.** Norvig pointed out that every two-word part of the sentence had appeared in published text before 1955. **Pereira (2001)** trained a statistical finite-state model, with word categories, on newspaper text.","sub":["That model computed that the grammatical order is 200,000 times more probable than the reversed order.","Many engineering solutions in natural language processing work this way."],"end":"So statistics over experience can capture a judgment Chomsky treated as needing an internal grammar."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p027","m1-lecture06-p030","m1-lecture06-p031"],"main":"**Two traditions in psycholinguistics differ in what they assume linguistic knowledge is.** The **GOFAI, modular or Chomskyan** tradition assumes knowledge is abstract, symbolic and different in kind from other knowledge. Its job is to study how that knowledge interfaces with messy perception, motor control and memory.","sub":["The **emergentist or functionalist** tradition assumes rule-like behavior emerges from statistical learning, and that perception and cognition shape linguistic representations.","Its job is to study language use and relate it to general psychology.","Messiness is everywhere, from probabilistic phonetic categories to ambiguous words, past tense exceptions like RUN and RAN, and syntactic variation.","**Moravec’s paradox** says theorem proving is easy for computers but recognizing a face is hard, while the opposite is true for humans."],"end":"So characterizing language in formal terms, as for a computer program, may be a false trail."},{"refs":["m1-lecture06-p032","m1-lecture06-p033","m1-lecture06-p034"],"main":"**Fixed categories and rules are often fuzzy in practice.** Wittgenstein (1953) noted that games share no single defining feature. Instead they form **family resemblances**, overlapping similarities that cross from one member to another.","sub":["Many phenomena in language are probabilistic in the same way.","Roger Shepard studied perception, Eleanor Rosch studied categorization, and Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky studied decision making. Their results made cognition seem less formal and rational."],"end":"So similarity-based generalization and hunches became serious alternatives to formal rules."}],"questions":[{"q":"Explain Marr’s three levels using the cash register.","a":"At the computational level, a cash register determines the amount due and the change. At the algorithmic level, it does this with addition and subtraction. At the implementational level, the hardware does not matter, so a card reader or a program can be a cash register too.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p016","m1-lecture06-p015"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is the difference between a Type I and a Type 2 problem, and why does it matter for language?","a":"A Type I problem breaks down cleanly, so its computation can be studied without its implementation. A Type 2 problem is solved by many interacting processes whose interaction is its own simplest description. If language is Type 2, brain implementation becomes unavoidable in a theory of language.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p017","m1-lecture06-p018","m1-lecture06-p020"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What did Elman’s simple recurrent networks learn from predicting the next item?","a":"A letter-prediction network found word boundaries in unspaced text, because its prediction error dropped within words. A network trained on simple sentences grouped words into grammatical classes and some semantic categories. Both results arose as side effects of prediction, not from built-in rules.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p022","m1-lecture06-p023","m1-lecture06-p024"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Contrast the two traditions in psycholinguistics.","a":"The GOFAI or Chomskyan tradition treats linguistic knowledge as abstract, symbolic and unlike other knowledge, and studies its interface with perception and memory. The emergentist tradition treats rules as emerging from statistical learning shaped by perception and cognition. It studies language use and relates it to general psychology.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p027"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-speech-errors","title":"Speech errors as evidence and the kinds of slips","tier":"Possible","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p002","m1-lecture07-p003"],"main":"**Speech errors are evidence about the units and processes of speech planning.** Fromkin titled her article “Speech errors as linguistic evidence.” This was gutsy for a linguist, because errors belong to performance rather than the idealized knowledge linguists usually study.","sub":["Spreading activation models explain errors with four ideas.","**Noise** in the activation of different parts of the system can push the wrong item forward.","**Frequency** matters, because common words are easier to activate.","**Other sources of activation** include what you just said, what you plan to say, and what you are trying not to say.","**Similarity** matters, because activation spreads more easily between items related in meaning, syntax or sound."],"end":"So an error happens when a competing item gets more activation than the intended one."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p004"],"main":"**A tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state is partial activation of a word’s meaning and sound that falls short of the threshold for producing it.** In the study reviewed by Lise Abrams, people were asked for the word meaning goods traded illegally, which is “contraband.” Those in a TOT state then read a list containing words that shared the first, middle or last syllable of the target, or no syllable.","sub":["People resolved their TOT states most often after words sharing the first syllable, such as “contact.”","Priming helps because the shared sounds add activation to the missing word form."],"end":"So a related sound can push a partly active word over the threshold for speaking."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p005","m1-lecture07-p006"],"main":"**Slips of the tongue differ in the direction the misplaced sound travels.** In **anticipation**, a later sound comes in too early, as in “week long race” becoming “reek long race.” In **perseveration**, an earlier sound repeats too late, as in “John gave the boy” becoming “John gave the goy.” In **metathesis**, two sounds swap places, as in “keep a tape” becoming “teep a cape.”","sub":["Dr. Spooner supposedly once said “Mardon me padam, this pie is occupewed. Can I sew you to another sheet?”","Anticipations come from activation of what you are planning to say.","Perseverations come from activation that remains from what you just said."],"end":"So the direction of a slip points to the source of the competing activation."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p009"],"main":"**Fromkin’s cluster errors are evidence that single phonemes are units in speech planning.** When her paper was written, there were arguments about whether the phoneme was a unit of planning. It might have turned out that whole consonant clusters always move together, which would give errors like “clesh freer water.”","sub":["Instead, clusters get broken up all the time in production.","“Fish grotto” became “frish gotto,” where only the r of the gr cluster moved.","“Fresh clear water” became “flesh queer water,” where only the r and l swapped."],"end":"So single segments, not whole clusters, are what move in these errors."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p007","m1-lecture07-p008","m1-lecture07-p023"],"main":"**Slips of the ear are perceptual errors, and they reveal top-down processing.** A **mondegreen** is a misheard phrase, named for hearing “laid him on the green” as “Lady Mondegreen.” Word boundaries are ambiguous, as in “The good candy came anyways” and “The good can decay many ways.” So context matters, especially in songs where prosody helps less.","sub":["A **malapropism** is a perceptual error, because the speaker says exactly what they intend.","The speaker heard a word like “congenital” and decided the intended word was a more familiar one, “congenial.”","Many speech errors go undetected for the same top-down reasons.","Some “errors” are deliberate, like “The upper hand is on the other foot,” which is memorable because it blends two familiar expressions."],"end":"So our expectations fill in what we hear, which explains both mishearings and unnoticed slips."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why should speech errors be of interest to psycholinguists?","a":"Speech errors are of interest because their form reveals the units and processes of speech planning. Fromkin’s title, “Speech errors as linguistic evidence,” was gutsy because errors belong to performance, not idealized knowledge. Errors that break clusters apart, for instance, are evidence that phonemes are planning units.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p002","m1-lecture07-p009"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What four aspects of spreading activation help explain speech errors?","a":"Noise in the activation of the system can let the wrong item win. Frequency matters because common words are easier to activate. Other sources of activation, like what was just said or is planned next, add competitors, and similarity lets activation spread to related items.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p003","m1-lecture07-p006"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What is a tip-of-the-tongue state, and why might priming help?","a":"A TOT state is partial activation of a word’s meaning and sound that falls short of the threshold for producing it. Priming helps because words sharing the target’s sounds add activation to its form. Resolution was highest after words sharing the first syllable of the target.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p004"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Distinguish anticipation, perseveration and metathesis with an example of each.","a":"Anticipation brings a later sound in too early, as in “reek long race” for “week long race.” Perseveration repeats an earlier sound too late, as in “John gave the goy.” Metathesis swaps two sounds, as in “teep a cape” for “keep a tape.”","refs":["m1-lecture07-p005"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why are malapropisms perceptual errors rather than speech errors?","a":"They are perceptual errors because the speaker says exactly what they intend. The speaker earlier heard an unfamiliar word like “congenital” and decided it must have been a familiar one, “congenial.” This is top-down processing at work in perception.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p008"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-functional-level","title":"The production model and the functional level","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p010","m1-lecture07-p011"],"main":"**The basic model of speech production turns a message into speech through a series of levels.** The levels are **Message**, **Functional**, **Positional**, **Phonological Encoding** and **Speech Gesture**. An early version comes from Fromkin (1971), whose utterance generator runs from the “meaning” to be conveyed down to motor commands to the muscles.","sub":["The message level decides what to say.","The functional level selects lemmas and assigns them semantic roles.","The positional level builds a syntactic frame with slots for those lemmas.","Phonological encoding turns the result into a string of phonemes.","The speech gesture level executes that string as movements."],"end":"So each kind of speech error can be traced to the level where it went wrong."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p012","m1-lecture07-p013"],"main":"**The message level is what the speaker intends to convey, and it is hard to study experimentally.** Take “I won a bunch of money on the game Sunday.” You would say it differently to a fan of the losing team, to someone who ignores football, and in a tweet. The event stays the same while the message changes with the audience.","sub":["Some studies try to control the message with an elicitation paradigm, such as the picture book “Frog, where are you?”","Even then, the intended message can depend on age, culture and audience."],"end":"So the message level remains somewhat mysterious."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p014"],"main":"**At the functional level we select lemmas, not finished words.** A **lemma** is a word’s abstract identity before its grammatical form is fixed. The error “Where’d I bring it when I put it in” was meant to be “Where’d I put it when I brought it in.”","sub":["The verbs switched places, but the produced forms reflect a tense change applied later.","If the finished word “brought” had been selected, it would have moved as “brought.”","Instead the lemma BRING was selected and later expressed in the present form its new position required."],"end":"So word identity is chosen before its grammatical form."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p015","m1-lecture07-p016"],"main":"**People often say the opposite of what they mean because antonyms are similar at many levels.** Antonyms are semantically related and strongly associated, which is a perfect storm in the spreading activation framework. They can also take the same semantic roles and belong to the same word class.","sub":["Because they fit the same roles and slots, the rest of the production system can handle the wrong one smoothly.","So it is easy for activation to spread from a lemma to its antonym, and for the antonym to win."],"end":"So a big change in meaning can come out as a small, grammatical substitution."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p017","m1-lecture07-p018"],"main":"**A blend mixes two competing words into one form.** Lewis Carroll deliberately built “frumious” from “fuming” and “furious.” Spontaneous blends happen when two alternatives, such as “mainly” and “mostly,” are active at the same moment.","sub":["Blends happen “where there are a number of alternative possibilities.”","In speech production there are almost always alternative possibilities, so competition is a normal part of speaking."],"end":"So blends are direct evidence that more than one candidate is active during word selection."},{"refs":["m1-lecture07-p019","m1-lecture07-p020"],"main":"**The functional level slots lemmas into semantic roles.** In “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me” and “I have eaten the plums in the icebox,” the roles are the same. The verb is TO EAT, the Agent is ME and the Undergoer is PLUM.","sub":["Arranging these roles in different orders has syntactic consequences, such as whether ME comes out as “I” or “me.”","That arrangement happens later, at the positional level.","By the end of the functional level we have decided on a message, selected lemmas and associated them with semantic roles."],"end":"So we are still nowhere near putting a clause together, much less saying it out loud."}],"questions":[{"q":"Why can’t we say that we select “words” at the functional level?","a":"We cannot say this because of the error “Where’d I bring it when I put it in.” The speaker meant “Where’d I put it when I brought it in.” The verbs swapped, but bring appeared in the form its new slot required. So the abstract lemma BRING was selected, and its tense was applied later.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p014","m1-review-p036"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why do people so often say the opposite of what they mean?","a":"Antonyms are semantically related and strongly associated, so activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym. They also take the same semantic roles and belong to the same word class. So the rest of the production system handles the wrong word smoothly.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p016","m1-review-p037"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What has been decided by the end of the functional level?","a":"By the end of the functional level, the speaker has decided on a message to convey. Appropriate lemmas have been selected and associated with semantic roles such as Agent and Undergoer. No clause has been built yet, and nothing has been said out loud.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p020","m1-review-p039","m1-lecture07-p019"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why is the message level hard to study?","a":"It is hard to study because the same event is expressed differently depending on audience and purpose. Winning money on a game would be told differently to a losing fan, a non-fan, or in a tweet. Elicitation tools like “Frog, where are you?” control what people see but not what message they intend.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p012","m1-lecture07-p013"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"What do blends reveal about speech production?","a":"Blends reveal that two alternatives can be active at the same time during word selection. Material from both words, like “mainly” and “mostly,” ends up in one form. Since there are almost always alternatives in speech, competition is a normal part of speaking.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p018","m1-lecture07-p017"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-positional-level","title":"The positional level, phonological encoding and gestures","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p002","m1-lecture08-p003","m1-lecture08-p004"],"main":"**The positional level builds a syntactic frame with slots for the lemmas chosen at the functional level.** Take “Where’d I bring it when I put it in?” The functional level supplies two verbs, PUT and BRING, with I/ME as Agent and IT as Undergoer of both. The positional level must also express the temporal relationship between the verbs.","sub":["The frame is “Where’d I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?”","The first clause requires the base form, because of the question structure with an auxiliary verb.","The next clause requires the past form.","The error arose while the slots were being filled, so BRING landed in the base-form slot and came out as “bring.”"],"end":"So the frame decides the form of a word, and the lemma decides which word fills it."},{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p005","m1-lecture08-p006","m1-lecture08-p007","m1-lecture08-p008","m1-lecture08-p009"],"main":"**Stranded affixes stay in their slot while the roots move, so affixes must be represented separately.** In “a language needer learns,” meant as “a language learner needs,” two lemmas switched while the positional level stayed the same. The noun is a verb plus the derivational affix -er, so -er stayed in the noun slot and attached to NEED.","sub":["“He did that easy enoughly,” meant as “easily enough,” also has a stranded affix, because -ly makes an adjective into an adverb.","In this case something also went wrong while setting up the positional frame, so the adverb slot ended up second and enough received the -ly.","Errors produce non-words like “needer” and “enoughly” on the fly, which a lexicon of stored related words could not do."],"end":"So lemmas and affixes are represented separately, affixes have slots, and derived words can be built by a grammatical process."},{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p010","m1-lecture08-p011"],"main":"**Phonological encoding turns the lemmas, affixes and particles into an ordered string of phonemes.** A positional instruction like “add -er” still leaves sound work to do. Easy /’i.zi/ becomes easily /’i.zIl.i/, and enough /i.’nəf/ becomes enoughly /i.’nəf.li/, while vowels and stress can shift as in certification.","sub":["In “easy enoughly,” phonological encoding worked fine, because the speaker did not say something like “easy /inəvɪli/.”","Errors at this level are problems with the ordering of phonemes.","Examples are “fish grotto” becoming “gish frotto” and “Chomsky and Halle” becoming “Chomsky and Challe.”","Another is “with this ring I do wed” becoming “with this wing I do red.”","Menn describes these errors as “neat,” because they follow English phonotactics and are clearly produced."],"end":"So phonological encoding errors are clean rearrangements of real phonemes."},{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p012"],"main":"**The speech gesture level turns the planned phonemes into movements executed by the motor system.** Unlike phonological encoding errors, gestural errors are not necessarily neat. They produce unclear or imprecise speech.","sub":["Many errors are ambiguous, such as “ad hoc” /æd hɑk/ coming out as “odd hack” /ɑd hæk/.","It could be metathesis at the phonological encoding level, or a miscuing of tongue position at the gestural level."],"end":"So a neat error points to encoding and a sloppy one points to gestures, but some errors fit both."},{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p014","m1-lecture08-p015","m1-lecture08-p017","m1-lecture08-p018","m1-lecture08-p019"],"main":"**Collected errors can be sorted by the level of representation that changed.** “Buyed in” for “bought in” regularizes an irregular past tense. “Wash my teeth and brush my face” exchanges two verbs while their objects stay put. “Following vowel” becoming “vollowing fowel” exchanges initial consonants. “Seat heaters” becoming “heat seaters” moves seat and heat while the ending stays with the second word.","sub":["“Lana del Rey” becoming “Marina del Rey” substitutes a familiar name.","“For all intensive purposes” replaces part of a familiar expression, like a malapropism.","“Law and Order SUV” reorders the letters of SVU into a familiar abbreviation.","“He cooked his kitchen” replaces chicken with a word that shares its sounds and fits the cooking context."],"end":"So placing an error in the model means comparing the intended and produced forms at each level."},{"refs":["m1-lecture08-p020","m1-lecture08-p021"],"main":"**Observational studies of speech errors are limited by the observer’s attention and perception.** What collectors listen for, and their expectations from the model, can change what they hear and report. Reconstructing an error also needs information about the situation, such as the intended message.","sub":["People are not very good at hearing sounds “halfway” between two phonemes, especially consonants.","People are biased against perceiving phonotactically illegal strings.","People are biased toward hearing ambiguous sounds as words, so a sound between “giss” and “kiss” sounds more like “kiss.”","This is **theory-dependent observation**, so whole classes of errors might be missed."],"end":"So experimental methods and careful measurement are needed, though they fix only some of these problems."}],"questions":[{"q":"What happens at the positional level?","a":"The positional level builds a syntactic structure with slots for the lemmas from the functional level, plus the particles the grammar requires. It also fixes the form each slot needs, such as base form or past tense. Errors like “Where’d I bring it when I put it in” arise while these slots are being filled.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p004","m1-review-p041"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How is “a language needer learns” similar to and different from “Where’d I bring it when I put it in”?","a":"It is similar because two lemmas switched while being fed into the positional level, and the frame stayed the same. It is different because the noun “needer” is a verb plus the derivational affix -er. The -er stayed in its slot and attached to the wrong root.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p006","m1-review-p042"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How is “He did that easy enoughly” similar to and different from “a language needer learns”?","a":"Both errors have a stranded affix, here -ly, which turns an adjective into an adverb. The difference is that something also went wrong while setting up the positional frame itself. The adverb slot ended up second, so enough received the -ly and easy lost it.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p008","m1-review-p043"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What do errors like “needer” and “enoughly” suggest about how words are represented?","a":"They suggest that lemmas and affixes are represented separately and that affixes have slots at the positional level. They also suggest that derived words can be produced by a grammatical process. A lexicon of stored related words could not create these non-words on the fly.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p009"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"How do phonological encoding errors differ from speech gesture errors?","a":"Phonological encoding errors misorder phonemes but are neat, following English phonotactics and produced clearly. Gesture errors happen in motor execution, so they produce unclear or imprecise speech. Some errors, like “odd hack” for “ad hoc,” could come from either level.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p011","m1-lecture08-p012"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why are observational collections of speech errors biased?","a":"They are biased because observers hear what their expectations and perception allow. Listeners miss sounds halfway between phonemes, resist hearing illegal strings, and hear ambiguous sounds as words, like “kiss” for something between “giss” and “kiss.” This theory-dependent observation can hide whole classes of errors.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p021","m1-lecture08-p020"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-frequency-confound","title":"Frequency, length and confounds in experimental design","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p002"],"main":"**Oldfield and Wingfield (1965) found that people name pictures of common objects faster than pictures of rare ones.** They asked people to name pictures of objects. Naming time fell steadily as the frequency of the name increased, in a **log-linear** relationship (a straight line when frequency is on a log scale).","sub":["But frequency is related to word length, even in a tiny sample of objects.","Frequent names tend to be short, so either frequency or length could explain the faster naming."],"end":"So the frequency effect started out **confounded** with length, meaning the two varied together."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p006","m1-lecture09-p003","m1-lecture09-p004","m1-lecture09-p005","m1-lecture09-p007"],"main":"**In English, frequency drops off steeply and length goes up as frequency goes down.** The 200 most frequent words in an old corpus were put into ten bins of twenty words. Mean frequency drops off very dramatically from the most frequent bin to the next, which is **Zipf’s law** in action.","sub":["Length is inversely related to frequency, so the most frequent words are the shortest.","A lexical database lets researchers generate word lists with set frequency and length limits, or look up the properties of an existing list.","Boxplots compare the lengths of the 467 most frequent words with 467 low-frequency words chosen at random.","Fewer than a quarter of the high-frequency words are as long as the median low-frequency word."],"end":"So choosing low-frequency words at random will always give longer words."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009"],"main":"**The solution is to match the two groups on length while still varying frequency.** Researchers should not select the low-frequency words at random. They pick longish high-frequency words and shortish low-frequency words, so the groups have the same lengths but different frequencies.","sub":["Oldfield and Wingfield eventually matched their high- and low-frequency words for length.","They still found a robust frequency effect.","So they concluded that frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise."],"end":"So much of psycholinguistics is an effort to show that an effect is not just an effect of some other confounding variable."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p010"],"main":"**Careful matching on many dimensions is part of good stimulus design, but it has costs.** Cutler (1981) called making up materials “a confounded nuisance.” **Cortese and Simpson (2000)** matched their words on a wide range of properties.","sub":["Fine-grained matching may select odd items whose statistics are unusual.","The frequency and length confound is a fact about English, so avoiding it creates a somewhat artificial set of words.","Matching can also leave very few items, and that study had only ten words per cell."],"end":"So matching removes a confound but can make the stimuli less representative of the language."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p011","m1-lecture09-p012"],"main":"**Most interesting psycholinguistic effects happen below conscious awareness, so tasks can be simple.** A person naming line drawings is not thinking about word frequency or length. So psycholinguists need less trickery than researchers studying stereotyping or altruism, and norming studies simply ask people to rate words.","sub":["Many measures are things participants do not consciously control, such as eye movements, EEG and fMRI.","Brain activity is useful as a **dependent variable** (the measured outcome), not only for what it says about the brain.","If two kinds of stimuli produce different brain responses even without a task, the stimuli are different to the participant.","Reaction time and accuracy are also unlikely to be under direct control in most experiments."],"end":"So good measures capture processing that participants cannot strategically change."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p030"],"main":"**A good idea for an experiment starts by asking whether an effect could just be something else.** The question is “Couldn’t it just be …?” Many features of words are correlated with frequency. These include length, age of acquisition, concreteness or imageability, and “contextual diversity.”","sub":["So frequency effects might be a catch-all for these other properties.","Even a simple blocking study involves several processes, including object recognition and response selection."],"end":"So each alternative explanation suggests a new experiment to rule it out."}],"questions":[{"q":"What did Oldfield and Wingfield first find, and why was it a problem?","a":"Oldfield and Wingfield (1965) found that pictures with more frequent names were named faster, in a log-linear relationship. The problem was that frequent words also tend to be short. So the frequency effect was confounded with length, and length could have explained it.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p002","m1-review-p045"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why will random selection of low-frequency words always produce a length confound?","a":"It will because length is inversely related to frequency in English. High-frequency words are generally shorter, and fewer than a quarter of them are as long as the typical low-frequency word. So random low-frequency words will almost always be longer.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p006","m1-lecture09-p007","m1-review-p046","m1-review-p047"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How do you separate frequency from length, and what happened when Oldfield and Wingfield did it?","a":"You choose longish high-frequency words and shortish low-frequency words so that the groups match in length but differ in frequency. Oldfield and Wingfield eventually matched their words this way. They still found a robust frequency effect, so frequency effects are not just length effects in disguise.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009","m1-review-p048"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What are the risks of very fine-grained stimulus matching?","a":"Fine matching may pick odd items whose statistics are unusual. Because confounds like frequency and length are real facts about English, avoiding them creates an artificial set of words. It can also leave very few items, such as ten words per cell.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p010"],"tier":"Possible"},{"q":"Why are measures like eye movements and brain activity valuable dependent variables?","a":"They are valuable because participants do not consciously control them. If two kinds of stimuli produce different brain responses even without a task, the stimuli must be different to the participant. Reaction time and accuracy are also rarely under direct control.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p012"],"tier":"Possible"}]},{"id":"m1-semantic-blocking","title":"Cyclic semantic blocking and lemma selection","tier":"On the review deck","blocks":[{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p014","m1-lecture09-p018","m1-lecture09-p019"],"main":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) found that people name pictures more slowly when the set is semantically related.** In the **cyclic semantic blocking** paradigm, participants repeatedly name sets of objects. **Homogeneous** (HOM) sets have related names, like duck, mouse, fish and snake. **Heterogeneous** (HET) sets have unrelated names, like duck, saw, chair and bus.","sub":["The experiment ran for eight cycles, and every picture was shown in each cycle.","The same item, such as duck, appeared in HOM and HET blocks in alternating cycles.","There is a big **identity priming effect**, because naming gets much faster after the first cycle.","There is also a **semantic blocking effect**, because HOM naming stays slower than HET naming after the first cycle."],"end":"So repetition speeds naming while semantic relatedness slows it."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p016","m1-lecture09-p017","m1-lecture09-p015"],"main":"**Spreading activation can slow naming because activation and selection are different processes.** A naïve view predicts that a block of similar pictures should speed naming. But naming requires selecting the correct lemma from a set of active ones.","sub":["Activation spreads from duck to fish, so related lemmas all become active.","The **WEAVER++** model says selection time depends on the ratio of the most active lemma’s activity to the total activation of all lemmas.","Feedback from the category level adds activation to all the category members, so the target is a smaller share of the total.","So lemma selection takes longer in a homogeneous block.","WEAVER++ focuses on single words, so its syntactic level holds things like number and gender rather than a positional frame."],"end":"So the same spreading activation that helps recognition can create competition in production."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p020"],"main":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian ruled out visual similarity as the cause of blocking.** Objects in the same category might simply look alike. First, the stimuli were normed so that same-category objects were not more visually similar, on average, than objects from different categories.","sub":["Second, a control experiment blocked visual similarity alone and found a different pattern.","In that control, the effect appeared in the first block, while semantic blocking appeared only after the first cycle."],"end":"So the blocking effect in Experiment 1 is not likely to come from visual similarity."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p021","m1-lecture09-p022","m1-lecture09-p023","m1-lecture09-p024","m1-lecture09-p025","m1-lecture09-p026"],"main":"**In Experiment 2, eye tracking located the blocking effect at lemma selection.** Hermens and colleagues had shown several objects at once and found no effect. So Belke, Meyer and Damian showed four objects simultaneously and had people name them in order while their eyes were tracked.","sub":["**Gaze duration** is how long the eyes stay on an object, and lemma selection is coordinated with eye movements.","**Lag** is the time between the eyes leaving an object and its name starting, when phonological encoding and gesture planning continue without visual support.","Speech duration got shorter over the experiment but showed no blocking effect, which fits the gesture level.","Gaze duration showed a blocking effect, but only after the first cycle, which fits lemma selection.","Lag showed no blocking effect, and its small reverse difference was not significant, which fits phonological encoding."],"end":"So the semantic cost shows up only in the measure tied to choosing the word."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p027"],"main":"**In Experiment 3, the blocking effect generalized to new items from the same categories.** New items were introduced in cycle five. Overall naming slowed down dramatically, and the repetition priming effect went away.","sub":["But the blocking effect was still there for the new items.","Comparing the new items with the items in cycle one separates category history from repeating a particular item."],"end":"So blocking comes from the category being active, not from repeating specific items."},{"refs":["m1-lecture09-p028","m1-lecture09-p029"],"main":"**Converging controls isolate semantic blocking at lemma selection.** It is not visual recognition, because the stimuli were normed and matched and a control experiment gave a different pattern. It is probably not phonological encoding, because the words are not phonologically similar and gaze duration, not lag, was affected.","sub":["It is not just a specific effect of repeating lists, because it generalizes to new items."],"end":"So each control removes one alternative, and together they leave lemma selection as the best explanation."}],"questions":[{"q":"Describe the cyclic semantic blocking design and its two main effects.","a":"Participants repeatedly named pictures over eight cycles in homogeneous sets with related names or heterogeneous sets with unrelated names. Naming got much faster after the first cycle, which is identity priming. Homogeneous sets stayed slower than heterogeneous sets after the first cycle, which is semantic blocking.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p018","m1-lecture09-p019","m1-review-p050"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How can spreading activation make naming slower instead of faster?","a":"Naming requires selecting one lemma from all the active ones. In WEAVER++, selection time depends on the target’s activation relative to the total activation of all lemmas. Related pictures and category feedback add activation to competitors, so the target is a smaller share and selection slows.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p017","m1-lecture09-p016","m1-review-p051"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"What did eye tracking in Experiment 2 reveal about where blocking happens?","a":"Gaze duration showed a blocking effect after the first cycle, and gaze is coordinated with lemma selection. Lag, which reflects phonological encoding after the eyes move on, showed no significant effect. Speech duration, linked to the gesture level, showed no blocking, so the cost sits at lemma selection.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p025","m1-lecture09-p026","m1-review-p054"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"Why does Experiment 3 matter?","a":"Experiment 3 matters because blocking survived when new items from the same categories were introduced. Repetition priming disappeared for the new items, but the semantic blocking effect remained. So blocking is not an item-specific effect of repeating lists.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p027","m1-review-p055"],"tier":"On the review deck"},{"q":"How did Belke, Meyer and Damian rule out visual similarity?","a":"They normed the stimuli so that same-category objects were not more visually similar than objects from different categories. They also ran a control experiment that blocked visual similarity. That control produced an effect in the first block, a different pattern from semantic blocking.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p020","m1-review-p056"],"tier":"On the review deck"}]}],"review":{"title":"Midterm 1 review guide","lead":"Every prompt on the Midterm 1 review deck, in order, with an answer written the way it could be said on the exam. Theme headings are turned into the question they ask you to answer, and a few predicted questions change one detail of a review prompt.","items":[{"n":1,"source":"Review deck p3, Theme 1","q":"What is a phoneme, and how do we know which sounds are phonemes in a language?","a":"A phoneme is the smallest phonetic unit in a language that can convey a distinction in meaning. We know two sounds are different phonemes when a minimal pair separates them, so swapping them changes meaning. Phonemes are not letters and not even specific sounds, because one phoneme can have several allophones.","refs":["m1-review-p003","m1-lecture01-p026","m1-lecture01-p019"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":2,"source":"Review deck p4","q":"How is the IPA consonant chart organized by features?","a":"The chart is organized by place, manner and voicing. Place of articulation runs along the x-axis from the front to the back of the vocal tract. Manner runs down the y-axis by degree of closure, from closed to open. Voicing separates the members of each pair by whether voicing starts before, during or after the closure.","refs":["m1-review-p004","m1-lecture01-p009"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":3,"source":"Review deck p5","q":"Compare /p/ and /b/ in terms of features.","a":"/p/ and /b/ have the same manner and place, because both are bilabial plosives. They differ in voicing, which is the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure. The same difference separates /t/ and /d/, so features make it possible to talk in general about the voiced stops /b/, /d/ and /g/.","refs":["m1-review-p005","m1-lecture01-p010"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":4,"source":"Review deck p6","q":"How do we establish that a speaker really has two different low back vowels?","a":"We have to find two words whose difference in meaning depends only on the difference between those vowels. That is a minimal pair, such as CAUGHT and COT for a speaker from the outer boroughs of New York City. If two phones are contrastive in a minimal pair, they are different phonemes.","refs":["m1-review-p006","m1-lecture01-p019","m1-lecture01-p017"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":5,"source":"Review deck p7","q":"How do minimal pairs show that English contrasts depend on a particular feature?","a":"English has minimal pairs for /b/ and /p/, /t/ and /d/, and /k/ and /g/. In each pair, place and manner stay the same and only voicing differs. So these phonemic contrasts all depend on voicing, and this is known from comparing minimal pairs within English.","refs":["m1-review-p007","m1-lecture01-p020"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":6,"source":"Review deck p8","q":"Why is it so important that phonemes are not letters?","a":"Literate people easily imagine speech as an orderly stream of letter-like units, but it is nothing like that. A spectrogram of “A sore back is a real pain” is a continuously changing signal with no clean breaks between sounds. Neighboring sounds overlap, so phonemes are categories we identify, not separate pieces in the signal.","refs":["m1-review-p008","m1-lecture01-p027"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":7,"source":"Review deck p9","q":"Explain Hockett’s (1955) Easter egg analogy.","a":"Raw eggs of various sizes and colors ride a belt into a wringer that smashes them together. The eggs stand for the series of phonemes, and the mess stands for the speech signal. An inspector examines the mess and decides which eggs went in, and he represents the hearer. He only has to identify the eggs, not put them back together.","refs":["m1-review-p009","m1-lecture01-p029"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":8,"source":"Review deck p10, Theme 2","q":"What are morphemes, and how do derivational and inflectional morphology use them?","a":"Morphemes are the meaningful parts that words are built from. Derivational morphology combines them to create new words, such as recertification. Inflectional morphology changes the form of the same word to fit the sentence, such as knife and knives.","refs":["m1-review-p010","m1-lecture03-p005","m1-lecture03-p013"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":9,"source":"Review deck p11","q":"How is the word morphology derived, and what would we call a unit in text?","a":"Morphology is morph-, from the Greek for form, plus -ology, the study of, which gives “the study of the forms of words.” The same process gives morpheme, just as phone gives phoneme. Following that pattern, a unit in text is a grapheme, built from graph-, the first part of graphology, plus -eme. The “oops” about graphology is a reminder that derived meanings are only partly predictable.","refs":["m1-review-p011","m1-lecture03-p005","m1-lecture03-p006"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":10,"source":"Review deck p12","q":"How does -hood show that derivation is productive but limited?","a":"The suffix -hood means a state of being associated with the stem noun, as in brotherhood and sisterhood. It is not applied evenly, because each knight has his own knighthood while brothers share their brotherhood. Neighborhood uses -hood for a different job, and we say friendship rather than friendhood for no apparent reason.","refs":["m1-review-p012","m1-lecture03-p007"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":11,"source":"Review deck p13","q":"Strong associates of “knife” can also be verbs. Are their meanings similar as verbs?","a":"No, their verb meanings are not similar. To “dish it out,” to have “a forked tongue” and to “table the issue” mean very different things. So even zero derivation, which only changes the part of speech, does complicated and unpredictable things with meaning.","refs":["m1-review-p013","m1-lecture03-p008"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":12,"source":"Review deck p14","q":"How can derivational morphology change the pronunciation of a word?","a":"Adding affixes can change the phonology of the rest of the word. Certificate, certification and recertification move the stress and change vowels. Record and invite change mainly in stress between noun and verb. This is one reason for “The Chaos” of English spelling.","refs":["m1-review-p014","m1-lecture03-p009"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":13,"source":"Review deck p15","q":"How does inflectional morphology change words, and why don’t we list inflected forms separately?","a":"Inflection changes meaning in a predictable way, marking plural, third person singular present and past tense. Knife becomes knives, walk becomes walks and walked, and sing becomes sang. As Menn puts it, separate dictionary entries for singular and plural nouns would be silly. The sound change can still be irregular, as in mice.","refs":["m1-review-p015","m1-lecture03-p010"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":14,"source":"Review deck p16","q":"How does inflection depend on syntax in a way derivation does not?","a":"In the phrase “two [noun]+pl,” whatever noun goes there must be pluralized somehow, so syntax requires the inflection. No syntactic process requires even a very productive derivational form. A formula like “Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it” is the closest case, but it is not exactly syntax.","refs":["m1-review-p016","m1-lecture03-p011"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":15,"source":"Review deck p17, Theme 3","q":"What is activation, and how does it spread in a lexical network?","a":"Activation is the graded level of activity of a node, such as a word, and it rises as evidence for that word grows. Nodes are linked by weighted connections set by association. So activating one word sends some activation to related words, which explains semantic and mediated priming.","refs":["m1-review-p017","m1-lecture04-p019","m1-lecture04-p028"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":16,"source":"Review deck p18","q":"How can words be represented in a simple lexical network?","a":"Each word is a node, such as LION, TIGER and STRIPES, that can be activated by that specific word. The node must respond to the word in any case, font or size, which is a lack of invariance problem. Its activation level stands for how strongly the word is currently represented.","refs":["m1-review-p018","m1-lecture04-p017","m1-lecture04-p018"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":17,"source":"Review deck p19","q":"What does it mean that activation can be partial?","a":"Activation can be partial when the evidence for a word is weak. When LION contrasts poorly with the noise mask, its node is only weakly activated. As the word becomes clearer, its activation increases.","refs":["m1-review-p019","m1-lecture04-p019","m1-lecture04-p020"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":18,"source":"Review deck p20","q":"What is the perceptual identification task, and how does the network model it?","a":"In the perceptual identification task, participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item. In the network, this happens when the signal-to-noise ratio is very favorable and activity is strong enough to count as identifying “lion.” So response time tracks how quickly activation reaches that point.","refs":["m1-review-p020","m1-lecture04-p021"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":19,"source":"Review deck p21","q":"How can activation dynamics explain frequency effects?","a":"Experience tunes up a word’s activation dynamics, so words met more often are easier to activate. Each encounter might raise the word’s baseline level of activation, or strengthen its incoming connections so it activates more quickly. It might also change a threshold parameter, and each of these is a very simple form of learning.","refs":["m1-review-p021","m1-lecture04-p024"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":20,"source":"Review deck p22","q":"Why does seeing a word again very shortly afterward lead to faster identification?","a":"Activation does not rise and fall instantaneously, and it falls off more slowly than it rises. If the word appears again while activation is still high, its baseline is already higher than usual. So it reaches identification sooner, and this is repetition priming.","refs":["m1-review-p022","m1-lecture04-p025"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":21,"source":"Review deck p23","q":"Why do neighborhood effects and related-word priming require adding something to the network?","a":"Both involve one word changing the activity of a different word. Neighborhood effects depend on how similar a word is to other words. Associative, semantic or phonological priming depends on a related word seen just before. So the nodes must be connected for activation to pass between them.","refs":["m1-review-p023","m1-lecture04-p026","m1-lecture04-p027"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":22,"source":"Review deck p24","q":"How does spreading activation represent the associations measured in norms?","a":"Connection strengths between nodes are determined by their association, as measured in the norms. When “lion” is activated, some of that activation travels along a weighted connection to “tiger.” So tiger is partly active before it appears, which is the spreading activation account of semantic priming.","refs":["m1-review-p024","m1-lecture04-p028","m1-lecture04-p012"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":23,"source":"Review deck p25","q":"What did Balota and Lorch test, and what did they find?","a":"They tested whether activation spreads automatically beyond directly associated concepts, using pairs like lion and stripes linked only through tiger. In lexical decision, directly related pairs helped but mediated pairs did not. In pronunciation, both directly related and mediated pairs helped, so activation does spread beyond direct associates.","refs":["m1-review-p025"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":24,"source":"Review deck p26","q":"What is mediated priming?","a":"Mediated priming is priming between two words that are not directly associated. Activity flows throughout the network, so activating “lion” spreads to “tiger” and then on to “stripes.” So lion can prime stripes through tiger.","refs":["m1-review-p026","m1-lecture04-p040"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":25,"source":"Review deck p27","q":"Why is the Balota and Lorch result representative of psycholinguistics, and why would “stripes” prime “lion”?","a":"It reflects careful study design and small effects, because the mediated benefit in pronunciation was only a little faster than neutral or unrelated primes. The result is counterintuitive, because stripes and lion are not directly associated. It happens because activation spreads through the shared neighbor tiger.","refs":["m1-review-p027","m1-lecture04-p041"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":26,"source":"Review deck p28","q":"How can spreading activation lead to slower naming times?","a":"A naïve view predicts that a block of similar pictures would speed naming. But related items spread activation to one another, so several competing lemmas are active at once. Selecting the right lemma from all the active ones takes longer, so naming slows.","refs":["m1-review-p028","m1-lecture09-p016"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":27,"source":"Review deck p29, Theme 4","q":"Explain Lichtheim’s house model of aphasia.","a":"Lichtheim’s house has a center for sound images (A), a center for concepts (B) and a center for movement images (M). An acoustic path brings sound in to A, and a motor path carries commands out from M. Specific aphasias come from damage to a center or a disconnection between centers. A break between A and B impairs understanding spoken words, and a break between A and M impairs verbatim repetition.","refs":["m1-review-p029","m1-lecture05-p007"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":28,"source":"Review deck p30","q":"Describe Broca’s patient Tan.","a":"In 1861, Broca presented “Tan,” who had lost the ability to speak after a brain injury decades earlier. Tan could only produce one jargon syllable. Otherwise he seemed to be in possession of his senses, and he seemed to understand what was said.","refs":["m1-review-p030","m1-lecture05-p005"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":29,"source":"Review deck p31","q":"What did Tan’s brain show, and what was Hughlings Jackson’s criticism?","a":"Tan’s brain had a large lesion definitely including the inferior frontal gyrus, the third convolution of the frontal lobe. This gave us Broca’s area, a part of the left frontal lobe important for speech. Hughlings Jackson (1874) objected that locating the damage that destroys speech and locating speech are two different things.","refs":["m1-review-p031","m1-lecture05-p006"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":30,"source":"Review deck p32","q":"How did Lichtheim and Wernicke explain specific losses of language function?","a":"They ascribed specific losses to disconnections between centers. A loss of understanding spoken words might come from a disconnection between A and B. A loss of verbatim repetition might come from a disconnection between A and M, and this center-based approach is an ancestor of modularity.","refs":["m1-review-p032","m1-lecture05-p007"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":31,"source":"Review deck p33","q":"What was Freud’s holist critique, and what tension does it raise?","a":"Freud criticized the diagram-drawing school as naïve for expecting the brain to be organized by psychological categories like word forms and concepts. He proposed that language emerged from fields connecting perceptual and motor abilities to drives. This raises the lasting question of whether brain areas map directly onto our descriptions of cognitive functions.","refs":["m1-review-p033","m1-lecture05-p008"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":32,"source":"Review deck p34","q":"What did Dronkers and colleagues find in Leborgne’s brain?","a":"Using MRI, they found the lesion was very deep and included the insula. There was also heavy white matter damage to the superior longitudinal fasciculus, which connects the front and back of the cortex. Other work by the group links speech apraxia, the condition Broca described, to the insula rather than Broca’s area.","refs":["m1-review-p034","m1-lecture05-p009"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":33,"source":"Review deck p35, Theme 5","q":"What happens at the functional and positional levels of speech production?","a":"At the functional level, the speaker selects lemmas and assigns them semantic roles such as Agent and Undergoer. At the positional level, a syntactic frame is built with slots for those lemmas and the particles the grammar needs. The slots also fix each word’s form, such as base form or past tense.","refs":["m1-review-p035","m1-lecture07-p020","m1-lecture08-p004"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":34,"source":"Review deck p36","q":"Why can’t we say we select “words” at the functional level?","a":"The error “Where’d I bring it when I put it in,” meant as “Where’d I put it when I brought it in,” is the evidence. The verbs switched places, but the produced forms reflect a tense change applied later. This could not happen if “brought” had been selected, so the lemma BRING was selected and later expressed in the present form.","refs":["m1-review-p036","m1-lecture07-p014"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":35,"source":"Review deck p37","q":"Why do people so often say the opposite of what they mean?","a":"Antonyms are similar at many levels, being semantically related and strongly associated. They can also take the same semantic roles and belong to the same word class. So activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym, and the rest of the production system handles the wrong word smoothly.","refs":["m1-review-p037","m1-lecture07-p016"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":36,"source":"Review deck p38","q":"How are lemmas slotted into semantic roles for “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me”?","a":"The event structure is the same in the passive and in “I have eaten the plums in the icebox.” The verb is TO EAT, the Agent is ME, the Undergoer is PLUM and the icebox is the Location. Arranging these roles in a particular order, and turning ME into “I” or “me,” happens later at the positional level.","refs":["m1-review-p038","m1-lecture07-p019"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":37,"source":"Review deck p39","q":"What has been accomplished by the end of the functional level?","a":"By the end of the functional level, we have decided on a message to convey. We have selected appropriate lemmas and associated them with semantic roles. We are still nowhere near putting a clause together, much less saying it out loud.","refs":["m1-review-p039","m1-lecture07-p020"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":38,"source":"Review deck p40","q":"For “Where’d I bring it when I put it in?”, what does the functional level supply, and what is left for the positional level?","a":"The functional level supplies two verbs, PUT and BRING, with I/ME as the Agent and IT as the Undergoer of both. What is left is the temporal relationship between the verbs and the question structure. Expressing all of this syntactically is the job of the positional level.","refs":["m1-review-p040","m1-lecture08-p003"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":39,"source":"Review deck p41","q":"What is in the positional frame for this sentence, and where does the error arise?","a":"The frame is “Where’d I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?” The first clause requires the base form, because of the question with an auxiliary verb, and the next clause requires the past form. The error arises while these slots are being filled with the lemmas from the functional level.","refs":["m1-review-p041","m1-lecture08-p004"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":40,"source":"Review deck p42","q":"How is “a language needer learns” similar to the bring and put error, and how is it different?","a":"It is similar because two lemmas got switched while being fed into the positional level, and the positional frame stayed mostly the same. It is different because the noun is actually a verb plus an affix. The derivational -er stayed in the noun slot and attached to NEED.","refs":["m1-review-p042","m1-lecture08-p006"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":41,"source":"Review deck p43","q":"How is “He did that easy enoughly” similar to “a language needer learns,” and how is it different?","a":"Both have a stranded affix, here -ly, which makes an adjective into an adverb. The difference is that something also went wrong while setting up the positional level itself. The adverb slot ended up second, so enough got the -ly and easy lost it.","refs":["m1-review-p043","m1-lecture08-p008"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":42,"source":"Review deck p44, Theme 6","q":"What is stimulus control in experimental design, and why does it matter?","a":"Stimulus control means choosing items that differ on the variable being tested but are matched on other properties that could affect responses. Without it, an effect can really come from a confounded variable. High-frequency words are generally shorter, so a frequency effect could be a length effect. Matching high- and low-frequency words for length rules this out, as Oldfield and Wingfield did.","refs":["m1-review-p044","m1-lecture09-p002","m1-lecture09-p008"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":43,"source":"Review deck p45","q":"What did Oldfield and Wingfield find, and what was the cautionary tale?","a":"Oldfield and Wingfield (1965) asked people to name pictures of objects. They found a log-linear relationship, with more frequent names produced faster. The cautionary tale is that frequency is related to word length, even in a tiny sample, so the result was confounded.","refs":["m1-review-p045","m1-lecture09-p002"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":44,"source":"Review deck p46","q":"What two patterns appear when the 200 most frequent words are put into ten bins?","a":"First, mean frequency drops off very dramatically from the most frequent bin to the next, which is Zipf’s law in action. Second, length is inversely related to frequency. So the most frequent words are also the shortest.","refs":["m1-review-p046","m1-lecture09-p006"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":45,"source":"Review deck p47","q":"What is the problem with selecting low-frequency words at random, and what is the solution?","a":"High-frequency words are generally shorter than low-frequency words, so random low-frequency words will always be longer. The solution is not to select them at random. Picking longish high-frequency words and shortish low-frequency words matches length while still manipulating frequency.","refs":["m1-review-p047","m1-lecture09-p008"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":46,"source":"Review deck p48","q":"What happened when Oldfield and Wingfield matched for length?","a":"They still found a robust frequency effect. So they concluded that frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise. Much of psycholinguistics is an attempt to show that an effect is not just an effect of some other confounding variable.","refs":["m1-review-p048","m1-lecture09-p009"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":47,"source":"Review deck p49, Theme 8","q":"What is cyclic semantic blocking, and what causes it?","a":"In cyclic semantic blocking, people repeatedly name sets of related or unrelated pictures, and related sets are named more slowly. Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) found the effect only after the first cycle. Their controls and eye tracking place the cost at lemma selection, where related competitors slow the choice.","refs":["m1-review-p049","m1-lecture09-p014","m1-lecture09-p028"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":48,"source":"Review deck p50 and p52","q":"Summarize the three experiments of Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005).","a":"Experiment 1 replicated semantic blocking and found it only after all objects in a set had been named once. Experiment 2 showed objects simultaneously and found blocking in naming latencies and gaze durations, but not in the gaze-to-speech lag. Experiment 3 found that blocking generalized to new, previously unnamed items. The authors proposed that the blocking effect comes from refractory behavior in the semantic system.","refs":["m1-review-p050","m1-review-p052","m1-lecture09-p014"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":49,"source":"Review deck p51 and p53","q":"Why must we distinguish activation from selection, and how does WEAVER++ explain blocking?","a":"Spreading activation from duck to fish means activating one helps activate the other. But the task is to select the correct lemma from many active ones. WEAVER++ assumes selection time depends on the ratio of the most active lemma’s activity to the total activation of all lemmas. Category feedback adds activation to every category member, so selection slows.","refs":["m1-review-p051","m1-review-p053","m1-lecture09-p017"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":50,"source":"Review deck p54","q":"How do speech duration, gaze duration and lag map onto stages of production?","a":"Speech duration fell over the experiment but showed no blocking effect, and it reflects the gesture level. Gaze duration showed a blocking effect after the first cycle, and it reflects lemma selection. Lag showed no significant effect, and it reflects phonological encoding, so blocking sits at lemma selection.","refs":["m1-review-p054","m1-lecture09-p026"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":51,"source":"Review deck p55","q":"Does blocking generalize to new items in the same category?","a":"Yes, the blocking effect generalized to new items introduced in cycle five. Overall naming slowed down dramatically and the repetition priming effect went away. But the blocking effect was still there, so it is not tied to repeating specific items.","refs":["m1-review-p055","m1-lecture09-p027"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":52,"source":"Review deck p56","q":"How do Belke, Meyer and Damian isolate the blocking effect in the model?","a":"It is not visual recognition, because the stimuli were normed and matched and a control experiment gave a different pattern. It is probably not phonological encoding, because the words are not phonologically similar and gaze duration, not lag, was affected. It is not just an effect of repeating lists, because it generalizes to new items.","refs":["m1-review-p056","m1-lecture09-p029"],"kind":"Review"},{"n":53,"source":"Predicted from review deck p5","q":"Compare /t/ and /d/, and then /d/ and /g/, in terms of features.","a":"The pair /t/ and /d/ shares place and manner and differs only in voicing, just like /p/ and /b/. Both /d/ and /g/ are voiced stops, so they share voicing and manner but differ in place of articulation. So any two of these stops can be described by which of the three features they share.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p010","m1-lecture01-p009","m1-lecture01-p020"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":54,"source":"Predicted from review deck p6","q":"For a speaker who says CAUGHT and COT the same way, does that pair show two vowel phonemes?","a":"No, for that speaker the pair does not show two vowel phonemes. A minimal pair only proves a contrast if the difference in sound changes meaning for that speaker. Most Americans outside the mid-Atlantic dialect say the two words alike, so they are homophones for them.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p017","m1-lecture01-p019"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":55,"source":"Predicted from review deck p15","q":"Is “knifed” an example of inflection or derivation, and how do you know?","a":"“Knifed” has two layers. Zero derivation first turned the noun knife into a verb, and the past tense -ed is then inflection. The -ed is inflection because it adds predictable grammatical meaning to the same verb.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p010","m1-lecture03-p004"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":56,"source":"Predicted from review deck p22","q":"Would repetition priming be larger after a short or a long delay between the two presentations?","a":"It would be larger after a short delay. Activation falls off gradually after the first exposure. So the sooner the word appears again, the more leftover activation it starts with, and the faster it is identified.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p025"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":57,"source":"Predicted from review deck p32","q":"In Lichtheim’s house, what would damage to the pathway from B to M predict?","a":"Damage between B and M would cut the route from concepts to movement images. So the person would have trouble turning ideas into speech. The path from A to M is spared, so verbatim repetition of heard words could still work.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p007"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":58,"source":"Predicted from review deck p42","q":"Analyze the error “seat heaters” becoming “heat seaters.”","a":"“Heat seaters” can be a stranded affix error, like “a language needer learns.” The stems seat and heat traded places, while the -ers ending stayed in the second slot. Because seat and heat differ only in their first consonant, it could also be a swap of those two sounds at phonological encoding. Either way, the ending stayed in its slot while the material before it moved.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p015","m1-lecture08-p011"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":59,"source":"Predicted from review deck p48","q":"Name another variable correlated with frequency that could confound a frequency effect, and how would you rule it out?","a":"Age of acquisition is correlated with frequency, along with length, imageability and contextual diversity. Any of these could be the real cause of a frequency effect. To rule one out, choose high- and low-frequency words that are matched on it, just as Oldfield and Wingfield matched on length.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p030","m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009"],"kind":"Predicted"},{"n":60,"source":"Predicted from review deck p54","q":"If semantic blocking had appeared in the eye-to-speech lag but not in gaze duration, what would that mean?","a":"It would place the blocking effect at phonological encoding rather than lemma selection. Lag covers the time after the eyes leave an object, when phonological encoding and gesture planning continue. The real result was the opposite, which is why blocking is placed at lemma selection.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p023","m1-lecture09-p026"],"kind":"Predicted"}]},"experiments":[{"who":"Hubel and Weisel (1968)","title":"Simple cells tuned to line orientation","asked":"They asked how the activity of single cells relates to visual input.","did":"They recorded simple cells in primary visual cortex (V1) while lines of different orientations moved through each cell’s receptive field.","found":"Each cell responded to lines of particular orientations in a particular receptive field. One cell fired most strongly to upward movement of a line at about 315°.","means":"A cell’s activity tells us what combination of features it represents. Psycholinguistics extends this idea of activation, as a metaphor, to word and concept representations.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p015","m1-lecture04-p016"]},{"who":"Balota and Lorch","title":"Mediated priming in pronunciation but not lexical decision","asked":"They asked whether activation spreads automatically beyond directly associated concepts in the memory network.","did":"They built prime and target pairs, like lion and stripes, that were related only through a mediating concept, tiger. People either made a lexical decision about the target or read it aloud.","found":"In lexical decision, directly related pairs like lion and tiger were faster, but mediated pairs were not faster than neutral or unrelated primes. In pronunciation, both directly related and mediated pairs were faster.","means":"Activation does spread beyond directly related concepts. The mediated effect is small, and features of the lexical decision task masked it.","refs":["m1-review-p025","m1-review-p027","m1-lecture04-p040","m1-lecture04-p041"]},{"who":"Broca (1861)","title":"The case of Tan","asked":"Broca asked what brain damage lay behind a patient who had lost the ability to speak.","did":"He presented the case of “Tan” to the Society of Anthropology and later examined Tan’s brain.","found":"Tan could produce only one jargon syllable but seemed to understand what was said. His brain had a large lesion that included the third convolution of the left frontal lobe, the inferior frontal gyrus.","means":"This region became Broca’s area, and the case made relating specific functions to specific brain regions a respectable pursuit. Hughlings Jackson (1874) warned that locating the damage that destroys speech is not the same as locating speech.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p005","m1-lecture05-p006","m1-lecture05-p011"]},{"who":"Flourens","title":"Experimental lesions against phrenology","asked":"Flourens asked whether specific parts of the cortex control specific senses, as phrenology implied.","did":"He made experimental lesions, mainly in pigeons and rabbits, and observed what was lost.","found":"He established distinct functions for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and midbrain structures. He found no match between specific cortical regions and specific senses, and when most of the cortex was removed, all senses were lost together.","means":"Flourens argued that the size of a cortical lesion mattered more than its location, which opposed the idea of separate sense centers.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p004"]},{"who":"Dronkers and colleagues","title":"MRI of Broca’s original patients","asked":"They asked exactly where and how far the damage extended in Broca’s landmark cases.","did":"They scanned the preserved brains, including Leborgne’s, with MRI.","found":"The lesion was very deep and included the insula, with heavy white matter damage to the superior longitudinal fasciculus. Other work by this group links speech apraxia to the insula rather than Broca’s area.","means":"Broca’s anatomical localization was crude and probably wrong, because the speech problem he described may depend on deeper structures and connections.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p009","m1-lecture05-p010"]},{"who":"Posner and colleagues","title":"PET subtraction for semantic processing of words","asked":"They asked whether operations like recognizing word forms and computing word meaning can be localized in the brain.","did":"They used PET with radio-labeled oxygen and subtracted control tasks from semantic tasks. Monitoring visual words for dangerous animals was compared with passive viewing, and generating uses was compared with repeating words, for visual and auditory input.","found":"All three semantic contrasts activated the left inferior prefrontal cortex, a region corresponding roughly to Broca’s area.","means":"Component operations on visual, phonological and semantic codes can be localized. Whole tasks like reading are not performed by any single brain area.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p015","m1-lecture05-p016","m1-lecture05-p017","m1-lecture05-p019"]},{"who":"Elman (1990)","title":"Simple recurrent networks that learn by prediction","asked":"Elman asked whether a network that only learns to predict the next item can capture aspects of language.","did":"He trained simple recurrent networks to predict the next letter in unspaced text, and the next word in simple sentences.","found":"The letter network’s prediction error went down within words, so it found word boundaries in text like “manyyearsagoaboyandgirl.” The word network grouped words into grammatical classes and some semantic categories.","means":"Word boundaries and word classes can emerge as side effects of prediction and gradual learning, without built-in rules.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p022","m1-lecture06-p023","m1-lecture06-p024"]},{"who":"Pereira (2001)","title":"A statistical model judges “colorless green ideas”","asked":"Pereira asked whether a statistical model can tell Chomsky’s grammatical but novel sentence from its reversed version.","did":"He trained a statistically based finite-state model, augmented with word categories, on newspaper text.","found":"The model computed that “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is 200,000 times more probable than “furiously sleep ideas green colorless.”","means":"Statistics learned from experience can separate a grammatical novel sentence from an ungrammatical one, which challenges Chomsky’s claim that this judgment needs an internal grammar.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p025","m1-lecture06-p014"]},{"who":"Lise Abrams","title":"Priming resolves tip-of-the-tongue states","asked":"The study asked whether hearing parts of a word helps people out of a tip-of-the-tongue state.","did":"People were asked for a word from its definition, such as goods traded illegally for “contraband.” Those in a TOT state read a list with words sharing the first, middle or last syllable of the target, or none. Then they tried again.","found":"People resolved their TOT states most often after words that shared the first syllable, like “contact.”","means":"A TOT state is partial activation that falls short of the threshold for speaking. Shared sounds add activation to the missing word form and push it over the threshold.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p004"]},{"who":"Fromkin (1971)","title":"Speech errors as linguistic evidence","asked":"Fromkin asked whether the phoneme is a real unit of speech planning, or whether whole consonant clusters move as units.","did":"She collected and classified spontaneous speech errors, including errors in words with consonant clusters.","found":"Clusters get broken up all the time. “Fish grotto” became “frish gotto,” and “fresh clear water” became “flesh queer water.”","means":"Single segments move independently of their clusters, so phonemes are units in speech planning. Her error data also supported a staged model of production.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p009","m1-lecture07-p002","m1-lecture07-p010"]},{"who":"Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)","title":"Word frequency and picture naming time","asked":"They asked how the frequency of an object’s name affects how quickly people name it.","did":"People named pictures of objects. Later, the researchers matched high- and low-frequency names for length.","found":"Naming time fell as frequency rose, in a log-linear relationship. After matching for length, there was still a robust frequency effect.","means":"Frequent names are usually short, so the first result was confounded with length. The matched result means frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p002","m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009"]},{"who":"Cortese and Simpson (2000)","title":"Matching word sets on many properties","asked":"They needed word sets that differ on the property of interest while being equal on everything else.","did":"They matched their word groups carefully on a wide range of lexical dimensions.","found":"The matching was very thorough, but it left only ten words per cell, which is small for a word naming experiment.","means":"Fine-grained matching removes confounds but can select odd items and create an artificial set of words.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p010"]},{"who":"Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)","title":"Experiment 1, cyclic semantic blocking","asked":"They asked why naming is slower for sets of semantically related pictures.","did":"Participants named pictures over eight cycles in homogeneous blocks of related items, like duck, mouse, fish and snake. Other blocks were heterogeneous, with unrelated items like duck, saw, chair and bus. The same item appeared in both kinds of block in alternating cycles.","found":"Naming got much faster after the first cycle, which is identity priming. Homogeneous blocks were slower than heterogeneous blocks, but only after all items had been named once.","means":"Repetition helps while semantic context hurts. In WEAVER++, related lemmas compete, so selecting the target takes longer.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p014","m1-lecture09-p018","m1-lecture09-p019","m1-lecture09-p017"]},{"who":"Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)","title":"Visual similarity control","asked":"They asked whether blocking might come from visual object recognition, because same-category objects can look alike.","did":"They normed the stimuli so that same-category objects were not more visually similar on average. They also ran a control experiment that blocked visual similarity alone.","found":"The visual control produced a different pattern, with an effect already in the first block.","means":"The semantic blocking effect, which appears only after the first cycle, is not likely to come from visual similarity.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p020","m1-lecture09-p028","m1-lecture09-p029"]},{"who":"Hermens and colleagues","title":"Simultaneous displays without a blocking effect","asked":"The study looked for semantic blocking when people saw several objects at once.","did":"Participants saw multiple objects simultaneously.","found":"They did not find a blocking effect.","means":"This raised the possibility that blocking depends on seeing objects one at a time, which Belke, Meyer and Damian then tested.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p021"]},{"who":"Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)","title":"Experiment 2, eye tracking during serial naming","asked":"They asked whether blocking survives simultaneous displays, and which stage of production it affects.","did":"Participants viewed four objects at once and named them in order while their eye movements were recorded. They measured gaze duration on each object, the lag from gaze leaving an object to its name starting, and speech duration.","found":"Gaze duration showed a blocking effect after the first cycle. Lag showed no blocking effect, and speech duration got shorter with practice but showed no blocking.","means":"Gaze duration is tied to lemma selection, so the semantic cost arises at lemma selection rather than phonological encoding or gestures.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p021","m1-lecture09-p022","m1-lecture09-p023","m1-lecture09-p025","m1-lecture09-p026"]},{"who":"Griffin, Bock and colleagues","title":"Eye and mouth coordination in describing events","asked":"Their demonstrations asked how looking is coordinated with speaking.","did":"A speaker described the scene “The horse is kicking a cow” during a similar-object name block while eye movements were recorded.","found":"The demonstration is labeled as accurately labeling a patient, the cow, during a similar-object name block.","means":"Eye movements can be lined up with the words a speaker produces, so where and how long people look can be used to study production.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p024"]},{"who":"Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005)","title":"Experiment 3, blocking with new items","asked":"They asked whether blocking generalizes to new items from the same categories.","did":"New items from the same categories were introduced in cycle five.","found":"Overall naming slowed down dramatically and the repetition priming effect went away, but the blocking effect remained.","means":"Blocking is not a specific effect of repeating lists. It comes from the category being active, which fits competition at lemma selection.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p027","m1-lecture09-p029"]}],"contrasts":[{"title":"Phoneme and allophone","sides":[{"name":"Phoneme","points":["The smallest unit in a language that can convey a distinction in meaning.","Proved by a minimal pair, such as CAUGHT and COT for a speaker who keeps them apart.","Written in slashes, as in /p/."]},{"name":"Allophone","points":["One of the different phones that realize the same phoneme.","No minimal pair separates it from the other realizations, as with [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ].","Written in square brackets, as in [pʰ] or [p̚]."]}],"why":"Two sounds can be clearly different to the ear and still be one phoneme. Only a change in meaning makes them separate phonemes.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p019","m1-lecture01-p023","m1-lecture01-p026","m1-lecture01-p012"]},{"title":"Broad and narrow transcription","sides":[{"name":"Broad transcription","points":["Uses slashes, as in /p/ and /b/.","Records the phonemes of an utterance.","Here voicing is a contrast that can be realized in several ways, such as aspiration or the timing in “writer” and “rider.”"]},{"name":"Narrow transcription","points":["Uses square brackets, as in [pʰ] and [p].","Includes as much detail about the actual pronunciation as possible.","Here a phone is voiced only if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole sound."]}],"why":"The two notations answer different questions, so the word voicing means something different in each.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p012","m1-lecture01-p024"]},{"title":"Derivational and inflectional morphology","sides":[{"name":"Derivational morphology","points":["Creates new words, such as the verb knife from the noun or certification from certificate.","Productive only in limited ways, so friendhood is not a word.","Meaning is only partly predictable, as with neighborhood.","Never required by syntax."]},{"name":"Inflectional morphology","points":["Changes the form of the same word, such as knife and knives or knife and knifes.","Highly productive, but not always regular, as in mice and sang.","Meaning is predictable, such as plural or past tense.","Required by syntax, as when “two” forces a plural noun."]}],"why":"The difference explains why “needer” in a speech error is a derived noun while the -s in “learns” is an inflection the frame requires.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p013","m1-lecture03-p011","m1-lecture03-p007","m1-lecture03-p010"]},{"title":"Syntactic roles and semantic roles","sides":[{"name":"Syntactic roles","points":["Describe grammatical positions such as subject and object.","The subject is the noun phrase that controls the verb.","Change between “I have eaten the plums” and “The plums were eaten by me.”"]},{"name":"Semantic roles","points":["Describe who did what to whom, such as Agent, Undergoer and Location.","The Agent is whoever performs the action.","Stay the same in both sentences, with me as Agent and the plums as Undergoer."]}],"why":"English usually puts the agent in the subject position, which is why “me” in a passive can wrongly feel like the subject.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p023","m1-lecture03-p024","m1-lecture07-p019"]},{"title":"Frequency effects and repetition priming","sides":[{"name":"Frequency effect","points":["Comes from how often a word has been met over a lifetime.","Explained by experience tuning up activation dynamics, through a higher baseline, stronger connections or a changed threshold.","A lasting property of the word."]},{"name":"Repetition priming","points":["Comes from seeing the same word very recently.","Explained by leftover activation, because activation falls off more slowly than it rises.","A short-lived effect of the experimental environment."]}],"why":"Both make a word easier to activate, but one reflects accumulated learning and the other reflects activation that has not yet decayed.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p024","m1-lecture04-p025","m1-review-p021","m1-lecture04-p023"]},{"title":"Direct semantic priming and mediated priming","sides":[{"name":"Direct priming","points":["Prime and target are directly associated, like lion and tiger.","Activation travels one step along a weighted connection.","Balota and Lorch found it in both lexical decision and pronunciation."]},{"name":"Mediated priming","points":["Prime and target are linked only through a third word, like lion and stripes through tiger.","Activation must travel two steps through the network.","Balota and Lorch found it in pronunciation but not in lexical decision."]}],"why":"Mediated priming is the stronger test of spreading activation, because no direct association can explain it.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p028","m1-lecture04-p040","m1-review-p025","m1-review-p026"]},{"title":"Bottom-up and top-down processing","sides":[{"name":"Bottom-up","points":["Activation flows from features to letters to words.","Driven by the input, so a clearer stimulus gives more activation.","Explains why LION is identified sooner as the noise mask fades."]},{"name":"Top-down","points":["Activation flows from words back down to letters.","Driven by stored knowledge, so an active word supports the letters that fit it.","Explains pattern completion of a partly hidden letter in WORK, and mishearings like malapropisms."]}],"why":"Recognition combines both directions, which is why expectations can fill in, or distort, what we perceive.","refs":["m1-lecture04-p042","m1-lecture04-p020","m1-lecture07-p008"]},{"title":"Tan and Wernicke’s patient","sides":[{"name":"Tan (Broca, 1861)","points":["Speech was almost absent, with only one jargon syllable.","Seemed to understand what was said.","Lesion included the left inferior frontal gyrus, later found to reach deep into the insula."]},{"name":"Wernicke’s patient","points":["Speech was fluent.","The speech was meaningless gibberish.","Studied in the 1870s, when Wernicke and Lichtheim explained aphasias as disconnections between centers."]}],"why":"Together the cases suggested that producing speech and producing meaningful speech can break down separately.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p005","m1-lecture05-p011","m1-lecture05-p007","m1-lecture05-p009"]},{"title":"Lesion studies and metabolic imaging","sides":[{"name":"Lesion studies","points":["Show what behavior looks like after a region is damaged.","Now located with anatomical imaging instead of waiting for autopsy.","Tell us only indirectly what the intact region does."]},{"name":"Metabolic imaging","points":["Studies the brain in action with PET or fMRI.","Measures an index of oxygen consumption, slowly and at millimeter resolution.","Needs a control condition and subtraction logic, because the brain is always active."]}],"why":"Both methods depend on well-defined cognitive operations, and neither escapes Hughlings Jackson’s warning that locating damage is not locating function.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p014","m1-lecture05-p015","m1-lecture05-p016","m1-lecture05-p013"]},{"title":"Behaviorism and the Cognitive Revolution","sides":[{"name":"Behaviorism (Skinner)","points":["Language is verbal behavior learned by operant conditioning.","New responses come from stimulus generalization along concrete similarity.","Speech is explained by stimulus control from the environment."]},{"name":"Cognitive Revolution (Chomsky)","points":["Language is an internalized grammar of abstract rules.","New sentences are recognized because the grammar generates them, not because they resemble old ones.","The tractable question is the structure of what people say, studied through grammaticality judgments."]}],"why":"Chomsky’s critique made internal states legitimate again, because only a grammar could explain judgments about an infinite number of new sentences.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p002","m1-lecture06-p010","m1-lecture06-p011","m1-lecture06-p013"]},{"title":"The GOFAI tradition and the emergentist tradition","sides":[{"name":"GOFAI, modular, Chomskyan","points":["Linguistic knowledge is abstract and symbolic.","Language is treated as a Type I problem, so the implementational level can be set aside.","The job is to study how abstract knowledge interfaces with perception, memory and action."]},{"name":"Emergentist, functionalist","points":["Rule-like behavior emerges from statistical learning.","Implementation matters, with distributed representations and gradual learning.","The job is to study language use and relate it to general psychology."]}],"why":"The traditions disagree about where an explanation of language should start, with an abstract grammar or with the system that learns and uses it.","refs":["m1-lecture06-p027","m1-lecture06-p017","m1-lecture06-p021"]},{"title":"Anticipation, perseveration and metathesis","sides":[{"name":"Anticipation","points":["A later sound comes in too early.","“Week long race” becomes “reek long race.”","Activation comes from what the speaker is about to say."]},{"name":"Perseveration","points":["An earlier sound repeats too late.","“John gave the boy” becomes “John gave the goy.”","Activation remains from what the speaker just said."]},{"name":"Metathesis","points":["Two sounds swap places.","“Keep a tape” becomes “teep a cape.”","Both sounds are active at once within the planned stretch of speech."]}],"why":"The direction of the slip reveals which source of activation won the competition.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p005","m1-lecture07-p006","m1-lecture07-p003"]},{"title":"Word exchanges and sound exchanges","sides":[{"name":"Lemma or word exchange","points":["Whole lemmas swap slots while the frame stays the same.","“Where’d I bring it when I put it in” or “a language needer learns.”","Affixes and tense stay in their slots and attach to the new root.","Arises while the positional level is being filled."]},{"name":"Sound exchange","points":["Single phonemes swap within or across words.","“Vollowing fowel” for “following vowel.”","The words stay in place and only their sounds move.","Arises at phonological encoding, and the result is neat."]}],"why":"Because the two kinds of exchange preserve different things, they are evidence for separate levels of lemmas and phonemes.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p004","m1-lecture08-p006","m1-lecture08-p011","m1-lecture08-p015"]},{"title":"Phonological encoding errors and speech gesture errors","sides":[{"name":"Phonological encoding error","points":["The phonemes are put in the wrong order.","Neat, because the result follows English phonotactics and is clearly produced.","Examples are “Chomsky and Challe” and “with this wing I do red.”"]},{"name":"Speech gesture error","points":["The motor system executes the planned phonemes imprecisely.","Not necessarily neat, so the speech comes out unclear.","“Odd hack” for “ad hoc” could be this kind or a metathesis."]}],"why":"Whether an error is neat or sloppy helps place it at the planning level or the movement level.","refs":["m1-lecture08-p011","m1-lecture08-p012"]},{"title":"Word frequency and word length as causes of naming speed","sides":[{"name":"Frequency","points":["How often a word is encountered.","Frequent names are named faster, in a log-linear relationship.","Still produced a robust effect when length was matched."]},{"name":"Length","points":["How long a word is.","Inversely related to frequency, so frequent words are short.","Was the confound that could have explained the original result."]}],"why":"Matching long high-frequency words with short low-frequency words separated the two, which is why frequency effects are not length effects in disguise.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p002","m1-lecture09-p006","m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009"]},{"title":"Activation and selection","sides":[{"name":"Activation","points":["Spreads from duck to related items like fish.","Helps recognition, so a related prime speeds identifying a word.","Produces identity priming, which speeds naming over cycles."]},{"name":"Selection","points":["Picks one lemma out of all the active ones.","In WEAVER++, depends on the target’s share of the total activation.","Produces semantic blocking, which slows naming in homogeneous sets."]}],"why":"The same spread of activation can speed recognition and slow production, because production must choose among competitors.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p017","m1-lecture09-p016","m1-lecture09-p019","m1-lecture04-p028"]},{"title":"Gaze duration and eye-to-speech lag","sides":[{"name":"Gaze duration","points":["How long the eyes stay on an object.","Tied to lemma selection.","Showed a blocking effect after the first cycle."]},{"name":"Eye-to-speech lag","points":["Time from the eyes leaving an object to its name starting.","Tied to phonological encoding and gesture planning, which continue without visual support.","Showed no significant blocking effect."]}],"why":"Because only gaze duration showed the cost, semantic blocking is placed at lemma selection.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p023","m1-lecture09-p025","m1-lecture09-p026"]}],"hard":[{"title":"Phonemes are not letters, and the IPA is not an alphabet of phonemes","trap":"Each IPA symbol, like each letter, stands for one phoneme.","text":"A phoneme is the smallest unit in a language that can convey a distinction in meaning. It is not a letter, and it is not even one specific sound. The IPA records phones, so [pʰ] and [p̚] get different symbols. In English both are the same phoneme /p/, because no minimal pair separates them. So the IPA describes sounds precisely, but which sounds count as phonemes depends on each language’s minimal pairs.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p026","m1-lecture01-p023","m1-lecture01-p012"]},{"title":"A sound difference you can hear is not automatically a new phoneme","trap":"Because aspirated [pʰ] and unaspirated [p] sound different, they must be different phonemes in English.","text":"Two phones are different phonemes only if swapping them changes meaning. There is no difference in meaning between [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ], and no minimal pair separates them in these contexts. So they are allophones of /p/. Even the English “voiced” stop in “beer” is often an unvoiced, unaspirated [p]. What makes it /b/ is its role in the contrast, not vocal fold vibration.","refs":["m1-lecture01-p023","m1-lecture01-p011","m1-lecture01-p024"]},{"title":"The subject is not always the agent","trap":"In “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me,” “me” is the subject because I did the eating.","text":"The subject is the noun phrase that controls the verb. In the passive sentence, “were eaten” agrees with plural plums, so the plums are the subject. “By me” is just a prepositional phrase inside the verb phrase. “Me” is still the Agent, because semantic roles stay the same in active and passive sentences. It feels like the subject only because English usually puts the agent first.","refs":["m1-lecture03-p023","m1-lecture03-p024"]},{"title":"A lemma is not a finished word","trap":"At the functional level the speaker selects finished words like “brought.”","text":"The intended sentence was “Where’d I put it when I brought it in.” The verbs swapped, and the speaker said “Where’d I bring it when I put it in.” If the finished word “brought” had been chosen, it would have moved as “brought.” Instead BRING appeared in the base form its new slot required. So the functional level selects the lemma BRING, and the positional level supplies its tense later.","refs":["m1-lecture07-p014","m1-lecture08-p004"]},{"title":"Spreading activation can slow naming","trap":"Since related words prime each other, naming a block of related pictures should be faster.","text":"Activation and selection are different processes. Spreading activation makes all the related lemmas, like duck, fish, mouse and snake, active together. In WEAVER++, selection time depends on the target’s activation relative to the total activation of all lemmas. Category feedback raises the competitors, so the target is a smaller share and selection takes longer. So homogeneous blocks are named more slowly after the first cycle, even though activation is spreading.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p016","m1-lecture09-p017","m1-lecture09-p019"]},{"title":"Where damage destroys a function is not where the function lives","trap":"Because Tan’s lesion was in the left inferior frontal gyrus, that region is where speech is located.","text":"Hughlings Jackson (1874) wrote that “to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things.” A lesion study shows what behavior looks like after damage, so it tells us only indirectly what the intact region does. In Tan’s case, MRI by Dronkers and colleagues found the damage reached deep into the insula and white matter. Their other work links Broca’s speech apraxia to the insula, not Broca’s area.","refs":["m1-lecture05-p006","m1-lecture05-p014","m1-lecture05-p009"]},{"title":"Frequency effects are not just length effects","trap":"Frequent words are named faster only because they are shorter.","text":"Frequent words are shorter, so the first frequency result was confounded with length. Picking low-frequency words at random will always give longer words. The fix is to pick longish high-frequency words and shortish low-frequency words, so length is matched while frequency differs. When Oldfield and Wingfield did this, they still found a robust frequency effect. So frequency matters on its own.","refs":["m1-lecture09-p002","m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009"]},{"title":"A missing mediated priming effect does not mean activation stops after one step","trap":"Because lion did not prime stripes in lexical decision, activation does not spread past directly related words.","text":"Balota and Lorch tested both lexical decision and pronunciation. In lexical decision, only directly related pairs like lion and tiger helped. In pronunciation, mediated pairs like lion and stripes also helped, compared with neutral and unrelated primes. So activation does spread through tiger to stripes. The effect is small, and features of the lexical decision task masked it.","refs":["m1-review-p025","m1-review-p027","m1-lecture04-p040"]}],"bytopic":{"m1-ipa-features":["m1-lecture01-p007","m1-lecture01-p008","m1-lecture01-p009","m1-lecture01-p010","m1-lecture01-p011","m1-lecture01-p012","m1-lecture01-p013","m1-lecture01-p014","m1-lecture01-p015","m1-lecture01-p016","m1-lecture01-p017"],"m1-phonemes-allophones":["m1-lecture01-p019","m1-lecture01-p020","m1-lecture01-p021","m1-lecture01-p022","m1-lecture01-p023","m1-lecture01-p024","m1-lecture01-p025","m1-lecture01-p026"],"m1-speech-signal":["m1-lecture01-p018","m1-lecture01-p027","m1-lecture01-p028","m1-lecture01-p029","m1-lecture01-p030"],"m1-morphology":["m1-lecture03-p004","m1-lecture03-p005","m1-lecture03-p006","m1-lecture03-p007","m1-lecture03-p008","m1-lecture03-p009","m1-lecture03-p010","m1-lecture03-p011","m1-lecture03-p012","m1-lecture03-p013"],"m1-phrases-roles":["m1-lecture03-p014","m1-lecture03-p015","m1-lecture03-p016","m1-lecture03-p017","m1-lecture03-p018","m1-lecture03-p019","m1-lecture03-p020","m1-lecture03-p021","m1-lecture03-p022","m1-lecture03-p023","m1-lecture03-p024","m1-lecture03-p025"],"m1-activation":["m1-lecture04-p002","m1-lecture04-p003","m1-lecture04-p004","m1-lecture04-p005","m1-lecture04-p006","m1-lecture04-p007","m1-lecture04-p008","m1-lecture04-p009","m1-lecture04-p010","m1-lecture04-p012","m1-lecture04-p013","m1-lecture04-p014","m1-lecture04-p015","m1-lecture04-p016","m1-lecture04-p017","m1-lecture04-p018","m1-lecture04-p019","m1-lecture04-p020","m1-lecture04-p021","m1-lecture04-p022","m1-lecture04-p023","m1-lecture04-p024","m1-lecture04-p025","m1-review-p021"],"m1-spreading-activation":["m1-lecture04-p026","m1-lecture04-p027","m1-lecture04-p028","m1-lecture04-p030","m1-lecture04-p031","m1-lecture04-p032","m1-lecture04-p033","m1-lecture04-p034","m1-lecture04-p035","m1-lecture04-p036","m1-lecture04-p037","m1-lecture04-p038","m1-lecture04-p039","m1-lecture04-p040","m1-lecture04-p041","m1-lecture04-p042","m1-lecture04-p043","m1-lecture04-p044","m1-lecture04-p045","m1-review-p025","m1-review-p027"],"m1-localization":["m1-lecture05-p002","m1-lecture05-p003","m1-lecture05-p004","m1-lecture05-p005","m1-lecture05-p006","m1-lecture05-p007","m1-lecture05-p008","m1-lecture05-p009","m1-lecture05-p010","m1-lecture05-p011"],"m1-brain-methods":["m1-lecture05-p013","m1-lecture05-p014","m1-lecture05-p015","m1-lecture05-p016","m1-lecture05-p017","m1-lecture05-p018","m1-lecture05-p019","m1-lecture05-p020"],"m1-cognitive-revolution":["m1-lecture06-p002","m1-lecture06-p003","m1-lecture06-p004","m1-lecture06-p005","m1-lecture06-p006","m1-lecture06-p007","m1-lecture06-p008","m1-lecture06-p009","m1-lecture06-p010","m1-lecture06-p011","m1-lecture06-p012","m1-lecture06-p013","m1-lecture06-p014"],"m1-marr-emergentism":["m1-lecture06-p015","m1-lecture06-p016","m1-lecture06-p017","m1-lecture06-p018","m1-lecture06-p019","m1-lecture06-p020","m1-lecture06-p021","m1-lecture06-p022","m1-lecture06-p023","m1-lecture06-p024","m1-lecture06-p025","m1-lecture06-p027","m1-lecture06-p030","m1-lecture06-p031","m1-lecture06-p032","m1-lecture06-p033","m1-lecture06-p034"],"m1-speech-errors":["m1-lecture07-p002","m1-lecture07-p003","m1-lecture07-p004","m1-lecture07-p005","m1-lecture07-p006","m1-lecture07-p007","m1-lecture07-p008","m1-lecture07-p009","m1-lecture07-p023"],"m1-functional-level":["m1-lecture07-p010","m1-lecture07-p011","m1-lecture07-p012","m1-lecture07-p013","m1-lecture07-p014","m1-lecture07-p015","m1-lecture07-p016","m1-lecture07-p017","m1-lecture07-p018","m1-lecture07-p019","m1-lecture07-p020"],"m1-positional-level":["m1-lecture08-p002","m1-lecture08-p003","m1-lecture08-p004","m1-lecture08-p005","m1-lecture08-p006","m1-lecture08-p007","m1-lecture08-p008","m1-lecture08-p009","m1-lecture08-p010","m1-lecture08-p011","m1-lecture08-p012","m1-lecture08-p014","m1-lecture08-p015","m1-lecture08-p017","m1-lecture08-p018","m1-lecture08-p019","m1-lecture08-p020","m1-lecture08-p021"],"m1-frequency-confound":["m1-lecture09-p002","m1-lecture09-p003","m1-lecture09-p004","m1-lecture09-p005","m1-lecture09-p006","m1-lecture09-p007","m1-lecture09-p008","m1-lecture09-p009","m1-lecture09-p010","m1-lecture09-p011","m1-lecture09-p012","m1-lecture09-p030"],"m1-semantic-blocking":["m1-lecture09-p014","m1-lecture09-p015","m1-lecture09-p016","m1-lecture09-p017","m1-lecture09-p018","m1-lecture09-p019","m1-lecture09-p020","m1-lecture09-p021","m1-lecture09-p022","m1-lecture09-p023","m1-lecture09-p024","m1-lecture09-p025","m1-lecture09-p026","m1-lecture09-p027","m1-lecture09-p028","m1-lecture09-p029"]},"wording":{"m1-lecture01-q01":{"college":"Why does a consistent romanization such as Pinyin still mislead an English reader?","simple":"Pinyin spells Mandarin in a regular way. Why can it still trip up an English reader?"},"m1-lecture01-q02":{"college":"Which consonant pair differs in voicing while sharing place and manner of articulation?","simple":"Which pair of sounds is made in the same spot and the same way, but differs in voicing (whether the vocal folds buzz)?"},"m1-lecture01-q03":{"college":"What relationship does the contrast between /p/ and [pʰ] represent?","simple":"What is the difference between /p/ and [pʰ] meant to show?"},"m1-lecture01-q04":{"college":"Moving from /i/ to /y/ by rounding the lips isolates which articulatory property of the vowel?","simple":"If you say /i/ and then round your lips to get /y/, which part of the vowel are you changing on its own?"},"m1-lecture01-q05":{"college":"Which feature of the F1 by F2 vowel plot demonstrates the lack of invariance problem?","simple":"On the vowel chart plotting F1 against F2 (two main resonance frequencies), what shows the lack of invariance problem (why sounds are hard to tie to fixed signals)?"},"m1-lecture01-q06":{"college":"For a speaker who distinguishes the vowels in caught and cot, what does this pair establish?","simple":"If someone says caught and cot with different vowels, what does that pair of words show?"},"m1-lecture01-q07":{"college":"In the English contexts given, why can final [p̚] and [pʰ] in stop be classified as allophones?","simple":"Why can the two ways of saying the last p in stop, [p̚] and [pʰ], count as versions of one sound (allophones)?"},"m1-lecture01-q08":{"college":"When an English speaker repairs an unfamiliar word-initial consonant cluster by inserting vowels, which concept accounts for the repair?","simple":"An English speaker adds vowels when trying to say a strange group of consonants at the start of a word. What best explains this?"},"m1-lecture01-q09":{"college":"Why do the phonetic labels aligned above a sentence spectrogram not correspond to discrete physical segments of the signal?","simple":"The sound labels above the sentence spectrogram (an image of sound frequencies over time) look like separate blocks. Why aren't the sounds really separate pieces?"},"m1-lecture01-q10":{"college":"According to Hockett’s egg analogy, what must the listener accomplish?","simple":"In Hockett’s egg comparison, what job does the listener have?"},"m1-lecture03-q01":{"college":"In He knifes him, why is knifes the correct form when the plural noun is knives?","simple":"Why do we say He knifes him, even though more than one knife is knives?"},"m1-lecture03-q02":{"college":"What do the -hood formations reveal about the productivity of derivational morphology?","simple":"What do the words ending in -hood show about how freely a word-building ending (a derivational suffix) makes new words?"},"m1-lecture03-q03":{"college":"Why does the series certificate, certification, and recertification illustrate morphology and phonology operating together?","simple":"Why are certificate, certification, and recertification good words for seeing word parts (morphology) and sound patterns (phonology) together?"},"m1-lecture03-q04":{"college":"Despite their different phonological changes, what morphological property do forks and mice share?","simple":"Forks and mice change in different ways. What do they have in common?"},"m1-lecture03-q05":{"college":"How does the requirement for a plural in two knives differ from the possibility of forming an un- derivative?","simple":"We must say two knives. How is that different from whether a word with un- can be made?"},"m1-lecture03-q06":{"college":"Why can two guys from the early ’nineties and a band with only one hit receive the same structural analysis?","simple":"Why can two guys from the early ’nineties and a band with only one hit be broken down the same way?"},"m1-lecture03-q07":{"college":"What information does a phrase-structure tree encode that a linear word list lacks?","simple":"What does a sentence tree (a phrase-structure tree) show that a plain list of words does not?"},"m1-lecture03-q08":{"college":"In The plums in the icebox were delicious, what determines the plural verb were?","simple":"Why is it were, not was, in The plums in the icebox were delicious?"},"m1-lecture03-q09":{"college":"Which constituent functions as the grammatical subject of The plums in the icebox were eaten by me?","simple":"In The plums in the icebox were eaten by me, which part is the subject (the part the verb agrees with)?"},"m1-lecture03-q10":{"college":"Which property is preserved when I have eaten the plums is passivized as The plums were eaten by me?","simple":"When I have eaten the plums turns into The plums were eaten by me, what stays the same?"},"m1-lecture04-q01":{"college":"Why is the pair TIGER and STRIPES an example of semantic association without similarity?","simple":"Why do TIGER and STRIPES go together even though they are not alike?"},"m1-lecture04-q02":{"college":"What evidence indicates that the V1 neuron is tuned to a conjunction of features?","simple":"What shows that the cell in V1 (the first visual area of the cortex) responds to a mix of features together?"},"m1-lecture04-q03":{"college":"Presenting LION in different fonts and letter cases illustrates which word-recognition problem?","simple":"LION is shown in many fonts, in capitals and small letters. What problem does this show for reading words?"},"m1-lecture04-q04":{"college":"What is the dependent measure in the perceptual identification task?","simple":"In the task where people try to identify a hard-to-see word, what gets measured?"},"m1-lecture04-q05":{"college":"How does an activation-based model account for the word-frequency advantage?","simple":"In a model where words get switched on by activation, why do common words have an edge?"},"m1-lecture04-q06":{"college":"Why does a recent prior presentation of a word facilitate its subsequent identification?","simple":"Why is a word recognized faster if you saw it just a moment ago?"},"m1-lecture04-q07":{"college":"What does assigning weights to connections contribute to a spreading-activation model?","simple":"In a model where activation spreads between words, what do connection weights (numbers set on each link) add?"},"m1-lecture04-q08":{"college":"Why does priming of LION by STRIPES qualify as mediated priming?","simple":"Why is STRIPES helping you recognize LION called mediated priming (priming between words that are not directly linked)?"},"m1-lecture04-q09":{"college":"In the interactive activation model, which influence operates top-down?","simple":"In the interactive activation example, which effect is top-down (flowing from higher levels down to lower ones)?"},"m1-lecture04-q10":{"college":"Why do overlapping distributed representations support generalization?","simple":"Why are overlapping distributed representations (patterns spread across many shared units) useful for generalizing (applying learning to new items)?"},"m1-lecture05-q01":{"college":"Which two weaknesses undermined phrenology as an account of localization?","simple":"What were the two main problems with phrenology (an old theory linking brain areas to traits)?"},"m1-lecture05-q02":{"college":"In interpreting large cortical lesions, what did Flourens stress?","simple":"When Flourens removed big parts of the cortex (the brain's outer layer), what did he focus on?"},"m1-lecture05-q03":{"college":"What behavioral dissociation made Tan’s case significant?","simple":"What mismatch in what Tan could and could not do made his case important?"},"m1-lecture05-q04":{"college":"What did Hughlings Jackson argue about inferring function from the location of damaged tissue?","simple":"What was Hughlings Jackson’s point about finding where the brain is damaged?"},"m1-lecture05-q05":{"college":"In Lichtheim’s house model, which disconnection would impair verbatim repetition?","simple":"In Lichtheim’s house-shaped model, which broken link would stop someone from repeating words back exactly?"},"m1-lecture05-q06":{"college":"What did MRI contribute to reinterpreting Broca’s historical patients?","simple":"What did modern MRI brain scans add to what we know about Broca’s old patients?"},"m1-lecture05-q07":{"college":"Why are quantitative cognitive measures valuable in studies relating lesions to behavior?","simple":"Why is it helpful to use tasks that give number scores when studying how brain damage affects behavior?"},"m1-lecture05-q08":{"college":"What does metabolic neuroimaging actually record?","simple":"What do brain scans based on metabolism (the brain's energy use) measure?"},"m1-lecture05-q09":{"college":"What is the rationale for contrasting a generate-uses task with word repetition?","simple":"Why compare thinking up uses for a word with just repeating the word?"},"m1-lecture05-q10":{"college":"Which statement is consistent with the component-operation view of functional localization?","simple":"Which idea fits the component-operation view of how the brain divides up work?"},"m1-lecture06-q01":{"college":"Why does the Turing test qualify as a behavioral criterion for machine intelligence?","simple":"Why is the Turing test called a behavioral test (one based on what can be observed) of machine intelligence?"},"m1-lecture06-q02":{"college":"What components must a formal problem representation supply to the General Problem Solver?","simple":"What does the General Problem Solver need in order to set up a problem?"},"m1-lecture06-q03":{"college":"Why do acceptability judgments about novel sentences matter for Chomsky’s argument?","simple":"Why does it matter to Chomsky that people can judge sentences they have never heard before?"},"m1-lecture06-q04":{"college":"What is controlled when “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is contrasted with its reversed ordering?","simple":"When “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is compared with its backward version, what stays the same?"},"m1-lecture06-q05":{"college":"In the cash-register example, at which level of analysis does adding item prices most directly belong?","simple":"In the cash register example, which level does adding up the prices belong to most directly?"},"m1-lecture06-q06":{"college":"What characterizes a Type II explanation?","simple":"What makes a Type II explanation different?"},"m1-lecture06-q07":{"college":"What role do the context units play in Elman’s simple recurrent network?","simple":"In Elman’s simple recurrent network (a network that reads a sequence one item at a time), what do the context units do?"},"m1-lecture06-q08":{"college":"In the unsegmented letter stream, what does declining prediction error within each word indicate?","simple":"When letters run together with no spaces, what does it mean that the network’s guessing errors drop inside each word?"},"m1-lecture06-q09":{"college":"How did grammatical and semantic clusters emerge in the network trained on sentence prediction?","simple":"How did the network that predicts the next word end up grouping words by grammar and meaning?"},"m1-lecture06-q10":{"college":"Which contrast captures Moravec’s paradox?","simple":"Which comparison sums up Moravec’s paradox?"},"m1-lecture07-q01":{"college":"When a speaker produces a sound belonging to the following word, which source of activation best accounts for its availability?","simple":"A speaker slips and uses a sound from the next word. Why was that sound ready to use?"},"m1-lecture07-q02":{"college":"For the target contraband in the tip-of-the-tongue study, which prime condition yields the greatest recovery rate?","simple":"When people are stuck on the word contraband (it is on the tip of their tongue), which kind of hint word helps them find it most often?"},"m1-lecture07-q03":{"college":"Intended “John gave the boy” is produced as “John gave the goy.” Which error type is this?","simple":"Someone means to say “John gave the boy” but says “John gave the goy.” What kind of slip is this?"},"m1-lecture07-q04":{"college":"What does mishearing “laid him on the green” as “Lady Mondegreen” reveal about speech perception?","simple":"What does hearing “laid him on the green” as “Lady Mondegreen” tell us about how we hear speech?"},"m1-lecture07-q05":{"college":"Someone deliberately says “congenial heart defect” because they learned the phrase that way. Which explanation fits?","simple":"A person means to say “congenial heart defect” because that is how they learned it. What explains this?"},"m1-lecture07-q06":{"college":"Why does the slip from “fish grotto” to “frish gotto” support segments as planning units?","simple":"Why does saying “frish gotto” for “fish grotto” show that we plan speech in single sounds (segments)?"},"m1-lecture07-q07":{"college":"In the slip from “Where’d I put it when I brought it in” to “Where’d I bring it when I put it in,” what is the critical evidence for lemmas?","simple":"Someone meant “Where’d I put it when I brought it in” but said “Where’d I bring it when I put it in.” What here shows lemmas (the stored word chosen for meaning and grammar)?"},"m1-lecture07-q08":{"college":"Why can an antonym compete strongly during lemma selection?","simple":"Why can a word with the opposite meaning get in the way when picking a word (lemma selection)?"},"m1-lecture07-q09":{"college":"How does the blend “swindged” differ from an exchange error?","simple":"How is the mixed word “swindged” different from two words swapping places?"},"m1-lecture07-q10":{"college":"What is preserved between “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me” and “I have eaten the plums in the icebox”?","simple":"What stays the same between “The plums in the icebox were eaten by me” and “I have eaten the plums in the icebox”?"},"m1-lecture08-q01":{"college":"How can exchanged verb lemmas surface in grammatically correct forms?","simple":"When two verbs swap places in a slip, why do they still come out in the right grammatical form?"},"m1-lecture08-q02":{"college":"Why is “a language needer learns” evidence about how morphology is assembled?","simple":"Why does the slip “a language needer learns” tell us how words are built from parts?"},"m1-lecture08-q03":{"college":"Under the proposed analysis, how does “easy enoughly” differ from the needer slip?","simple":"How is the slip “easy enoughly” different from the needer slip?"},"m1-lecture08-q04":{"college":"What theoretical value do novel forms such as enoughly have?","simple":"Why do made-up forms such as enoughly matter for theories of speaking?"},"m1-lecture08-q05":{"college":"Why does attaching a derivational affix still leave work for phonological encoding?","simple":"After a word-building ending (derivational affix) is added, why is there still work to do on how the word sounds?"},"m1-lecture08-q06":{"college":"A speaker clearly produces gish frotto for fish grotto. Which property favors a phonological-encoding account?","simple":"A speaker clearly says gish frotto instead of fish grotto. What about this slip points to the stage that plans word sounds (phonological encoding)?"},"m1-lecture08-q07":{"college":"Why is ad hoc produced as odd hack ambiguous about the production level at which it arose?","simple":"Why can’t we tell which stage of speaking caused ad hoc to come out as odd hack?"},"m1-lecture08-q08":{"college":"Why is buyed for bought particularly revealing about morphology?","simple":"Why does saying buyed instead of bought tell us so much about how words are built?"},"m1-lecture08-q09":{"college":"Why must the context of a spontaneous speech error be taken into account?","simple":"Why does it matter what was going on when someone made a slip?"},"m1-lecture08-q10":{"college":"In what way can perception bias a corpus of naturally occurring speech errors?","simple":"How can the way listeners hear things skew a collection of everyday speech slips?"},"m1-lecture09-q01":{"college":"In an unmatched comparison of naming latencies for high- and low-frequency words, why is word length a confound?","simple":"When comparing how fast people name common and rare words without matching them, why does word length get in the way (act as a confound)?"},"m1-lecture09-q02":{"college":"If a frequency effect persists after the word sets are matched for length, what does that support?","simple":"If common words are still named faster after both groups are made the same length, what does that show?"},"m1-lecture09-q03":{"college":"What cost can result from matching stimulus lists on many variables?","simple":"What is the downside of making word lists alike in many ways?"},"m1-lecture09-q04":{"college":"In WEAVER++, why can spreading activation slow picture naming?","simple":"In the WEAVER++ model, why can spreading activation make naming a picture slower?"},"m1-lecture09-q05":{"college":"Which comparison isolates the effect of semantic context on naming a target such as duck?","simple":"Which comparison tests only the effect of meaning-related context when naming duck?"},"m1-lecture09-q06":{"college":"How can repetition facilitation and semantic blocking both be observed within one experiment?","simple":"How can repetition facilitation (getting faster at naming a picture you named before) and semantic blocking (an effect of naming pictures from one category together) both show up in the same study?"},"m1-lecture09-q07":{"college":"How did the visual-similarity control condition inform the interpretation of semantic blocking?","simple":"How did the check using look-alike pictures help judge the semantic blocking effect?"},"m1-lecture09-q08":{"college":"In the multiple-object naming paradigm, how is eye-to-speech lag defined?","simple":"In the task of naming several objects, what is the eye-to-speech lag?"},"m1-lecture09-q09":{"college":"Which timing result supports locating semantic blocking at lemma selection?","simple":"Which timing pattern supports the idea that semantic blocking happens while picking the word (lemma selection)?"},"m1-lecture09-q10":{"college":"What makes the introduction of new items in cycle five diagnostic?","simple":"Why does adding new items in cycle five tell us something useful?"},"m1-review-q01":{"college":"Why does a minimal pair establish that two sounds belong to different phonemes?","simple":"Why does a minimal pair (two words that differ in only one sound) show that two sounds are separate phonemes?"},"m1-review-q02":{"college":"Why does the plural in “two forks” exemplify inflection?","simple":"Why is the plural in “two forks” a good example of inflection (endings like plural -s)?"},"m1-review-q03":{"college":"Within an activation model, what mechanism accounts for the word-frequency effect?","simple":"In an activation model, what explains why common words are recognized faster?"},"m1-review-q04":{"college":"What pattern of mediated priming did Balota and Lorch report?","simple":"What did Balota and Lorch find about mediated priming (priming through an in-between word)?"},"m1-review-q05":{"college":"What was the substance of Jackson’s objection to a simple localization account?","simple":"What was Jackson’s objection to a simple account of where functions sit in the brain?"},"m1-review-q06":{"college":"How does “Where’d I bring it when I put it in” support separating a lemma from its inflected form?","simple":"How does the slip “Where’d I bring it when I put it in” show that a stored word (lemma) is separate from its final form with endings?"},"m1-review-q07":{"college":"Relative to the intended “a language learner needs,” what does “a language needer learns” preserve?","simple":"Someone meant “a language learner needs” but said “a language needer learns.” What stayed the same?"},"m1-review-q08":{"college":"What was the purpose of matching high-frequency and low-frequency words on length?","simple":"Why did it matter to give common and rare words the same length?"},"m1-review-q09":{"college":"In the Belke study, which finding helped localize semantic blocking to lemma selection?","simple":"In the Belke study, which result helped show semantic blocking happens while picking the word (lemma selection)?"},"m1-review-q10":{"college":"Why was the introduction of new items in cycle 5 diagnostic for semantic blocking?","simple":"Why did adding new items in cycle 5 tell us something about semantic blocking?"}}};
